(project pricklypear-branching
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: branching")
 (status partial)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-02 status reconcile: agent-promote, package-updates, branch-browser-ui → implemented. Still open: schema-authority (type_defs merge), ast-nodes (design).|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section branching
  (doc "Branches as the fundamental organizational primitive: code branches, shared data, fork/merge")
  (status partial)
  
  (details
   (doc (|
        Branches are to Pricklypear what files are to
        Unix: the fundamental organizational primitive.
        Everything is a branch; everything else is built
        on top of that.

        STORAGE (one Postgres, not one DB per branch):
        - branches(id, parent_id, owner, name, ...)
        - function_defs / type_defs / view_bindings
          each keyed by (branch_id, name|route)
        - data_rows has NO branch_id — shared data
        No separate schemas/tables per branch. No
        Postgres INHERITS (see storage.no-inheritance).

        KEY DISTINCTION: branches are for CODE, not data.
        - BRANCHED: function definitions, type schemas,
          view/route bindings.
        - SHARED: data_rows (todos, notes, bible, …).
          Alice's experimental branch renders against
          REAL shared data. She forks code, not data.

        TWO FORK SHAPES:
        - EMPTY child (personal garden): parent pointer
          only; inheritance via recursive CTE. Main
          package updates stay visible unless overridden.
        - FULL fork (experiment): copy code-rows from
          parent (cheap snapshot). Parent updates do not
          appear until merge.

        ROLES:
          system/main     package defaults (inheritance root)
          <user>/personal garden (boot default; long-lived pins)
          <user>/dev      optional workshop (empty child of main;
                          active product work intended to promote)
          <user>/<name>   experiments (full fork when isolation
                          from evolving main is required)

        AUTHORITY (Nopales vs kernel) — see package-updates:
          Kernel (OCaml/migrations)  → git is source of truth
          Package Nopales               → Postgres branches are live
                                       source; git holds snapshots
          Boot seed                  → additive into system/main
                                       only (never clobber existing)
          Explicit reseed            → git libs may overwrite main
                                       when an admin asks

        Effective definition lookup (CLOS override):
        walk parent chain, closest definition wins.
        Writes (define/route) always hit the ACTIVE
        branch only.
   |))
  )
  (subsection fork
   (doc "Forking a branch")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         (fork 'main 'alice/experiment)

         Implementation:
         1. INSERT INTO branches (id, parent_id, owner,
            name, ...) VALUES (new_uuid, main_id, 'alice',
            'experiment', ...)
         2. INSERT INTO function_defs (branch_id, name,
            body, author, updated_at)
            SELECT new_uuid, name, body, author,
            updated_at FROM function_defs
            WHERE branch_id = parent_id
         3. Similarly for type_defs, view_bindings

         Cost: O(num code rows in parent). For ~100 fns
         and ~20 types, a few hundred row inserts. Lands
         in well under a second.

         Forking does NOT copy data table rows. The
         forked branch's functions operate on the same
         shared event rows as the parent.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml — 48 alcotest cases")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "fork creates a branch and copies function_defs")
    (smoke "override-walk resolves child overrides before parent; branches isolate writes"))

  )
  (subsection merge
   (doc "Merging a branch into its parent")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         (merge 'alice/experiment 'alice/main)

         Policy:
         - Default: admin-only merges to main,
           per-user can merge into their own branches
           freely.
         - Expandable later (PR/review flow).

         Implementation (Store.merge_branch, image/lib/store.ml):
         upserts all function_defs rows from source into target
         via INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (branch_id, name) DO UPDATE,
         wrapped in a SERIALIZABLE transaction. Initial cut
         copies the full set; diff-based merge (only changed
         rows, per-function conflict resolution) is not yet
         implemented.

         CONFLICT REPORT (v1: report-only, never blocks):
         before the upsert, merge computes names where BOTH
         source owns a row AND target owns a row whose
         updated_at is newer than source's (target moved
         independently since the lens diverged). Result
         string carries them:
           "merged N rows (M conflicts: a, b, c)"
         Resolution stays last-write-wins (source body lands);
         the report tells the human what was clobbered, and
         code-history makes the clobbered target body
         recoverable. Blocking/require-resolution merge is a
         later PR/review-flow concern, not v1.

         SCOPE: merge copies function_defs only. It MUST NOT
         copy type_defs (schema authority is main; see
         schema-authority) and MUST NOT copy view_bindings
         blindly (route hijack risk). Routes travel with
         named functions through promote instead
         (see promote).

         type_defs merge: not implemented by design in v1.
         A branch cannot propose a schema change through merge;
         schema changes land via migrations applied on boot
         (see migrations.additive-only) and are visible to all
         branches on next lookup.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml — fork/merge/promote alcotest cases")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "merge upserts changed rows; merges into system/* require admin")
    (smoke "merge result string lists conflict names when target moved newer")
    (smoke "merge copies no type_defs and no view_bindings rows"))
  )
  (subsection diff
   (doc "Structural divergence between two branches (AST-aware, not textual)")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/prims_db.ml image/lib/http_branches.ml lisp/lib/ast_diff.ml lisp/test/ast_diff_test.ml)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-16 ralph loop 19: implemented + verified. Store.diff_branches (store.ml), (diff from to) prim (prims_db.ml), GET /diff?from=&to= private page (http_branches.ml, http.ml route) wired; ast_diff 27/27 green, full dune build @all + runtest exit 0. 2026-08-14: gap surfaced testing personal-vs-main divergence. merge_branch is naive full-copy with explicit TODOs (diff-based merge, conflict detection); diff is the primitive those TODOs consume. AST-awareness chosen because function_defs.body is ONE expr node (not a statement list), JSONB-tagged structurally - see lisp-interpreter.ast-diff.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "lisp/test/ast_diff_test.ml — same/modified over all 11 real expr constructors (Var Lit App Let Letrec If Lambda Prim Seq Quote Define)")
    (test "App.args and Letrec.bindings use list-LCS keyed on analyze.ast_hash so insertion mid-sequence does not mark every following element changed")
    (test "top-level ast_hash equality short-circuits the walk for unchanged rows")
    (smoke "(diff 'system/main 'admin/personal) lists added/removed/modified names without scraping /bindings HTML")
    (smoke "/diff?from=system/main&to=admin/personal renders Modified rows with per-ctor AST deltas; Added/Removed show pp_expr of the lone body"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM: testing divergence between a personal
         branch and main is currently manual —
         (list-bindings) under each (checkout ...) and
         eyeballing. merge_branch (see merge) copies ALL
         function_defs source->target with three open
         TODOs: diff-based merge (only changed rows),
         conflict detection (parent moved same name
         since fork), and per-function resolution. diff
         is the primitive those TODOs consume, and the
         thing a human/agent reads before promoting.

         (diff 'system/main 'admin/personal)
           -> VList of change-dicts, one per divergent
              name (no row for unchanged — the common
              case):
              {name kind status from-updated to-updated}
              kind   :: fn | type | route
              status :: added | removed | modified

         LAYERING (two stages, at the seam the codebase
         already has):
           1. ROW CLASSIFICATION (SQL, one round trip):
              both effective views via the SAME recursive
              CTE that list_function_names_meta /
              list_packages / search_function_bodies use
              (closest-ancestor-wins). FULL OUTER JOIN on
              name, classify each as
                added    = in to,   not in from
                removed  = in from, not in to
                modified = both, AND from.body IS DISTINCT
                                       FROM to.body
              Scope mirrors merge_branch: function_defs +
              type_defs + view_bindings (NOT data_rows —
              shared, branch-free per branching.data).
              Conflicts fall out for free: this is the
              inverted conflict_sql already in merge_branch.
           2. AST DELTA (OCaml, Modified rows ONLY):
              parse both bodies via expr_of_json, walk with
              lisp/lib/ast_diff.ml (see lisp-interpreter.
              ast-diff). Never runs on Added/Removed
              (nothing to compare — show pp_expr of the
              lone body) or on Same (short-circuited).

         AST-AWARENESS IS A PROPERTY, NOT A NICETY.
         function_defs.body is ONE expr node (Lambda body,
         not a statement list), JSONB-tagged structurally
         ({"if":...}, {"lambda":...}, {"app":...}). A
         textual diff over the JSONB ::text would be
         unreadable; a line diff over pp_expr output
         would jitter on whitespace and miss structural
         moves. The diff is structural over the expr type:

           ctor differs (Var -> Lambda)            = Replaced
           same ctor, fixed-arity children          = Node,
             recurse positionally (If test/then/else;    each
             Seq a/b; Lambda params/body; Let name/      child
             val/body; Define name/val; Quote body;       its
             Var s; Lit v)                                own
                                                        delta
           same ctor, list children                = Node
             with a list-LCS step over the children      plus a
             before recursing into matched pairs:        moved/
             - App.args (head + args list)              added
             - Letrec.bindings (keyed on binding name)  list
                                                        handled
           leaf value differs (Var s, Lit v)        = Replaced

         The 11 real expr constructors (ast.ml): Var,
         Lit, App, Let, Letrec, If, Lambda, Prim, Seq,
         Quote, Define. The Quasiquote / Unquote /
         UnquoteSplicing mentioned in lisp-interpreter.
         ast are reader-level sugar that desugars before
         persistence (see macros) — they do not appear
         in function_defs.body and need no diff case.

         FAST PATH: analyze.ast_hash (sha1_hex of
         Reader.pp_expr) is already the content identity.
         Compare from/to hashes first; if equal, skip the
         walk entirely (returns Same). The 90% case
         (unchanged defs between branches) costs one hash
         compare per row, not a tree walk.

         SCOPE & TYPE/ROUTE HANDLING:
           - function_defs: full AST diff as above.
           - type_defs: diff the `fields` jsonb array
             with the same list-LCS (field declarations
             are positional lists, not expr trees; no
             ast_diff involvement).
           - view_bindings: tabular (route, handler)
             pair sets — divergent pairs are
             added/removed rows, no body to diff. Stays
             in SQL.
           Schema changes land via migrations (see
           schema-authority), never via diff/merge.

         NON-GOALS (v1, honest):
           - No rename detection. A bound-var rename
             x->y surfaces as a Replaced at the binding
             site (Lambda.params / Let name / Letrec
             binding name) AND a Replaced at every
             reference site (Var x -> Var y). git doesn't
             do this for free either; real rename
             detection needs the 3-way base (fork point),
             which is the natural v2 once code_history
             before-images + branches.last_synced are
             wired as the base.
           - No alpha-equivalence. Bound names are NOT
             normalized before comparing — a genuine
             rename is meant to be visible, not hidden.
           - Param order matters. (lambda (a b) ...) vs
             (lambda (b a) ...) is a positional child
             delta, NOT "renamed params".

         DEGRADED RENDER PATH: if the /diff page does
         not want to render the structured delta tree,
         fall back to a unified line diff over
         Reader.pp_expr from / Reader.pp_expr to. This is
         the existing simple_text_diff shape (see
         code-diff) applied to two live bodies instead
         of a history entry. Always available; never the
         primary surface.

         WIRE-IN:
           - Store.diff_branches ~from_id ~to_id
             (image/lib/store.ml, beside merge_branch):
             the SQL stage + Modified-row (from_expr,
             to_expr) pairs ready for the image layer.
           - (diff source target) prim (image/lib/
             prims_db.ml, beside code-diff): returns the
             VList of change-dicts (summary level). The
             structured AST delta is consumed in-process
             by the /diff page renderer (Ast_html +
             Ast_diff), NOT exposed as a separate prim
             in v1. A programmatic (diff-ast name from
             to) prim is a v2 addition if agents need
             the delta tree as Nopales values.
           - /diff?from=&to= private page (image/lib/
             http_branches.ml, sibling to /bindings):
             three-column row table; Modified rows
             expand to the AST delta overlaying
             Ast_html.pp_expr_html. Future "promote
             selected" off diff rows IS the merge TODO
             (diff-based merge) in disguise.

         DOWNSTREAM CONSUMERS (the point of building it):
           - merge TODO "diff-based merge (only changed
             rows)": merge calls diff, upserts only rows
             whose status != same.
           - merge TODO "conflict detection": the
             3-way base is the base_gen coordinate of
             branch-status (tree-time made it a READ,
             superseding the code_history / timestamp
             plan recorded here).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection branch-status
   (doc "Git-style clean/ahead/behind/diverged status as ONE derived read of the tree-time substrate")
   (status implemented)
   (implements migrations/024_branch_base_gen.sql image/test/branch_status_test.ml)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-28: implemented + verified same-day as the plan pass. Migration 024 (branches.base_gen + backfill-to-parent-current-gen approximation); fork/ensure write it; Store.branch_status (store.ml, one round trip, all three kinds, chain-walk over tree_versions + live-pin EXISTS on the scaffolding tables); classify_status decision table; /branches dirty+synced columns, branch detail status table (unbind hint on synced pins), /changes + (branch-changes) class = status; list_overrides DELETED (4 call sites); merge_branch status-driven (upserts only ahead-new/ahead/diverged; conflict report = the diverged set; base advance post-commit) + head-vs-head fallback for non-parent targets. TWO make-phase findings recorded in the details: (1) HASH DOMAIN - the stored tree_versions.hash column is NOT comparable across write paths (tree-first Tree_store.hash_body compact-JSON digests vs 009-mirror digest(jsonb::text) spaced-canonical digests: identical bodies, different hashes - 020 itself calls the stored hash "a consistency artifact, not content"), so status computes digest((body->'body')::text) AT QUERY TIME, one domain regardless of writer; a global rehash is tree-time scope, and gen-diff has the same latent cross-domain hazard when the two compared gens were written through different paths. (2) MERGE BASE ADVANCE MUST RUN POST-COMMIT - the 017/019 gen-bump triggers are deferred, so reading branch_generations inside the merge tx sees the pre-merge counter; the advance is a separate statement after with_transaction returns. Verified: image/test/branch_status_test (lifecycle: fork base_gen = parent head gen, empty fork clean, ahead-new, diverged, behind, post-promote synced, merge advances base_gen + post-merge synced; identical-pin-synced + unbind-returns-clean) + live smoke on the dev habitat (fork -> define -> ahead-new -> promote -> synced -> redefine -> diverged -> merge reports the conflict by name -> synced). Full dune runtest green; borge drift: none.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM: branch status is computed AD HOC, per
         surface, as name-presence joins on the
         scaffolding tables. Store.list_overrides
         classifies every own function_defs row as
         new | override | main newer by NAME EXISTENCE
         + updated_at heuristics — never by content.
         Consequences, all observed:
           - a full fork marks every function
             "override" though every body is identical
           - after promote, the source pin stays
             IDENTICAL to main but reads "override"
             (or "main newer", the timestamp flip)
             forever
           - nothing can answer "does this branch
             actually diverge?" without scraping /diff
           - merge's conflict heuristic is updated_at
             string comparison; there is no fork point
             anywhere in storage (branches.last_synced
             is an unused timestamp)
         No incremental fix works: each surface re-
         derives branch semantics itself. The fix is to
         stop deriving branch semantics from row
         geography and start deriving it from CONTENT
         at GENERATIONS — the tree-time substrate that
         already exists.

         READ PATH (2026-09-08 perf batch): b_res/p_res resolve
         from LIVE tree_nodes rows, not a full scan of every gen of
         every def on the chain (delete_fn physically removes the
         row; only the version trail keeps a tombstone, so live rows
         are tombstone-free by construction — the old tombstone-
         filtered DISTINCT ON over version heads is gone). a_res
         keeps the gen-bounded versions walk (historical base state,
         exact old semantics). CTEs are MATERIALIZED (live_all was
         recomputed per reference); ancestor-only key rows the OCaml
         filter dropped are no longer computed. branch_status is
         memoized per (branch, chain def_gen-SUM stamp) OUTSIDE
         transactions — sum not max: main's counter dwarfs a garden
         branch's, so a bump on the branch itself must move the
         aggregate; count catches tree_branches row
         appear/disappear, base_gen catches merge advances. Inside a
         transaction: never cached (deferred-mirror windows must read
         their own tx). Dev: /branches 8.4s cold → 0.17s warm, with
         live invalidation on define.

         MODEL (two coordinates, additive):
           - base_gen: NEW COLUMN on branches (bigint).
             At fork, base_gen = parent head gen
             (branch_generations.generation of the
             parent at fork time, parent chain walked;
             0 for branches forked before the column
             exists whose parent has no own writes).
             After a merge or promote lands on the
             parent, base_gen ADVANCES to the merged
             head — this is git's moving merge-base,
             and it RETIRES branches.last_synced
             (timestamp), which was designed for this
             job in the code_history era and never
             wired.
           - head gens: already counted per branch by
             branch_generations (migration 017), the
             same counter tree-time promoted to a
             storage key.

         Because tree_versions (018-021 shadow) is
         append-only with a content hash per version
         row, "value of path P on branch B at gen G"
         is a READ, not a snapshot: newest version row
         for (resolved-owner-of-P, P) with gen <= G,
         non-tombstone. Resolution for uncopied gens
         uses the same closest-wins branch-lens rule as
         head reads.

         THE STATUSES (per name, fn | type | route):
           b = branch value at own head,
           p = parent-chain value at parent head,
           a = value at base_gen
           (absence = tombstone/never-existed;
            comparisons are HASH comparisons, ast_hash
            for fn bodies per diff's fast path):
             clean      no own write since base,
                        b == p (inherited; may differ
                        from a — that is normal
                        upstream movement, not dirt)
             ahead-new  own write; parent has never
                        had the name (a absent)
             ahead      own write; b != a, p == a
             behind     no own write since base;
                        p != a  (upstream moved)
             diverged   own write; b != a AND p != a
                        AND b != p  (the genuine
                        conflict set merge reports)
             synced     own write; b == p  (identical
                        pin — post-promote or package-
                        pin noise; the unbind candidate
                        class, replaces "override")
         Git mapping: clean = up to date, ahead*
           = committed-locally, behind = remote moved,
           diverged = conflict, synced = cherry-picked
           downstream already. "dirty" = anything but
           clean|behind (own writes exist).

         ONE COMPUTATION:
           Store.branch_status ~branch_id
           -> rows {name kind status branch_hash
                    parent_hash base_gen head_gen}
           Hash-first per tree-time: compare hashes,
           prune on equality; bodies are fetched ONLY
           for rendering (Modified rows go through
           lisp/lib/ast_diff.ml exactly as /diff does
           today). One round trip per path class, same
           recursive-CTE chain walk style as
           diff_branches — but against tree_versions,
           not function_defs.

         CONSUMERS (every current status surface
         becomes a rendering of branch_status, none
         keeps its own classifier):
           - /branches list: "overrides" count becomes
             dirty count (ahead-new + ahead + diverged);
             synced pins counted separately, muted
           - branch detail: Overrides table gains the
             status column; synced rows offer unbind
             as the primary action
           - /changes + (branch-changes): class is the
             status, not new|override|own
           - /diff + (diff ...): same hash pairs; the
             added/removed/modified classification is
             a view of the same rows (ahead = modified
             in to, absent in from at matching gens)
           - merge: upserts ONLY ahead/ahead-new/
             paths (diff-based merge TODO
             closes); DIVERGED paths are NEVER
             auto-applied — they come back as the
             conflict report and resolve only by an
             explicit re-define + re-merge (obituary
             rule: no silent reversals; LWW resolution
             retired 2026-09); on success source and
             target base_gens advance
           - promote: after landing, offer unbind of
             synced pins on the source

         RETIRES:
           - Store.list_overrides (4 call sites: the
             three /branches renderers + branch-changes;
             no Nopales package depends on it)
           - the "main newer" timestamp badge
           - branches.last_synced (timestamp) — its job
             is base_gen's, done correctly
           - diff_branches' scaffolding-table CTEs
             become historical the day PP_TREE_READS
             stabilises; during coexistence BOTH the
             versioned status and the classic diff prim
             must agree (verifier below) — status is a
             NEW consumer of the shadow table, not a
             read-switch of the scaffolding

         POSITION IN THE BOOK: this is a tree-time
         consumer (tree-time.consumers gains a line),
         sibling to code-history's re-implementation
         over tree_versions. It does NOT require the
         tree-time Phase-2 scaffolding read-switch:
         tree_versions is maintained by the dual-write
         shadow today, so reading it is additive.
         Scope is code paths only (b.* / fn-type-route);
         data rows are branch-free by the one-tree
         DECISION and have no branch status to compute.

         PHASES:
           S1  migration: ALTER branches ADD base_gen +
               backfill; fork writes it; merge/promote
               advance it. Store.branch_status over
               tree_versions + one equivalence verifier
               (sample N branches: statuses vs
               diff_branches head-vs-head where both
               defined).
           S2  surfaces re-render: /branches, branch
               detail, /changes, branch-changes prim,
               /diff alignment. list_overrides deleted
               in this phase, not before.
           S3  merge + promote consume the statuses;
               diverged = conflict report only, never
               auto-applied; base_gen advancement tested
               end-to-end (fork -> edit both sides ->
               merge -> status clean again = git's
               post-merge state).
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test/branch_status_test.ml — lifecycle: fork sets base_gen = parent head gen; merge advances base_gen")
    (test "statuses: empty fork = clean; own new fn = ahead-new; promote-then-status = synced; parent-only edit = behind; both-sides edit = diverged; identical pin = synced; unbind returns it to clean")
    (test "equivalence: diff_branches reports the diverged fixture as modified head-vs-head, agreeing with branch_status")
    (smoke "S2: /branches renders dirty + synced columns; branch detail status table with unbind hint on synced; (branch-changes) class = status; list_overrides has no callers")
    (smoke "S3: merge upserts only ahead/ahead-new names; diverged names are conflict-report-only, never auto-applied (merged 0 rows when all synced); base_gen advances post-commit"))
  )

  (subsection store-split
   (doc "The store split: store.ml was a ~2,700-line monolith mixing five domains and the conn/tx substrate into ONE module")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-09 split i-1788671083-c, pinned by scripts/verify_store_split.sh: store.ml became a composition facade over store_common (rel names + the ONE branch-chain CTE + staleness stamp), store_defs (def lookups/counts/fingerprints), store_branch (branch status/persist/promote/merge/diff/fork), store_packages (package registry), store_history (code history/diff/restore), store_retention (tree-time.gc retention worker). Two structural decisions the script pins: (1) the facade does NOT re-export the substrate wholesale — explicit accessors instead of 'include Store_db'; (2) the whole family stores OPAQUE jsonb bodies — no store_*.ml module references the Lisp interpreter (the AST codec lives at the eval boundary: Pricklypear_lisp.Ast.expr_of_body_json). row.ml is the shared sibling: ONE pgx row-cell codec that previously existed as byte-identical copies inside ai_store/jobs/media/auth_store (i-1788671505).|) *)
   (implements
    image/lib/store.ml
    image/lib/store_common.ml
    image/lib/store_defs.ml
    image/lib/store_branch.ml
    image/lib/store_packages.ml
    image/lib/store_history.ml
    image/lib/store_retention.ml
    image/lib/row.ml
    scripts/verify_store_split.sh
    scripts/verify_store_chain_sql.sh)
   (verify
    (build "ksh scripts/verify_store_split.sh")
    (smoke "facade exposes explicit accessors only: renaming a family function breaks the build until the accessor is updated")
    (smoke "deleting one family prim registration and re-running the verifier FAILS (negative test)"))
  )

  (subsection db-prim-split
   (doc "The DB-prim split: prims_db registered 137 prims across unrelated domains; the families now live in per-domain modules with prims_db as composition root")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-09 split i-1788671048/1035, pinned by scripts/verify_prims_db_split.sh: the old single-closure prims_db.register registered 137 prims across unrelated domains; the families now live in prims_data / prims_sync / prims_jobs / prims_auth / prims_code / prims_agent / prims_llm / prims_ops, with prims_db as composition root (registers NOTHING) over shared helpers in prims_db_common. The script pins the full 137-name list (no drops/renames/duplicates), the server-only set of exactly 7 names (remote-eval classification stays registry-derived per prim-seam.server-endpoint), and that every family module exposes 'let register'. Each family's BEHAVIOR is owned by its domain chapter (data -> one-tree; sync -> local-first; jobs -> jobs.borg; auth -> auth.borg; code history -> branching.code-history; agent/llm -> glochid.borg); this stanza declares the MODULE decomposition so the drift surface has a home.|) *)
   (implements
