  (subsection agent-promote
   (doc "Agent promotes from its workshop into the parent human's personal")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: promote_to_personal + allow_agent_promote user toggle + set-allow-agent-promote prim; agent boots parent/agent workshop.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         promote(source=<parent>/agent, target=<parent>/personal):
         Selective upsert of function_defs from the agent's
         workshop into the parent human's personal branch (same
         mechanism as promote_to_main, different target). The
         agent's work lands in the human's garden.

         Permission: gated by the parent human's allow_agent_promote
         toggle (users table, default true). The human can revoke
         it to freeze the agent out of their personal branch; the
         agent still works in <parent>/agent but cannot promote.

         This is the human-in-the-loop gate: the agent builds in
         its own branch; nothing reaches the shared tree
         (system/main) or the human's garden until the agent
         promotes and the human allows it. promote to system/main
         remains admin-only.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection schema-authority
   (doc "Types in main, branches read against main's schema")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02 confirm partial: shared data_rows + additive migrations; type_defs merge still not implemented (details admit).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Schema authority is 'main'. Branches cannot change
         the underlying table shape directly; schema changes
         land via additive-only migrations applied on boot
         (see migrations.additive-only), and are visible to
         all branches immediately on next function lookup.

         Data is keyed by user/space/lens, not by branch (no
         branch_id on rows); type_defs are stored
         per-branch but the underlying table shape is shared
         (see object-model). Branch code that ignores new
         fields reads a subset without error. Branch code
         that expects removed fields would fail, but
         additive-only means removed fields do not occur.

         type_defs merge: not yet implemented. Today all
         data lives in the shared data_rows table; per-type
         Postgres tables are not created by deftype.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection personal-workspace
   (doc "Boot on empty <user>/personal; seed package defaults only onto system/main")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         MODEL:
         - system/main = package defaults (inheritance root).
           Boot seed_stdlib writes libs/*.pp here only,
           under seed-policy (additive — see below).
         - <user>/personal = garden (user channel).
           Empty child of system/main (parent pointer only).
           Image boots here after seed. User (define) and
           (route) write personal rows only.
         - <user>/dev = optional workshop (also empty child).
           Prefer for product spikes you intend to promote;
           keep personal for long-lived custom pins.
         - <parent>/agent = agent user's default workshop.
           Empty child of system/main (sibling of the parent
           human's personal). An agent user boots here, not into
           the parent's personal. The agent works in its own
           branch and promotes into the parent's personal when
           the human allows it (see promote).
         - Inheritance: recursive CTE; child overrides
           shadow main; unpinned names auto-see main updates.

         BOOT ORDER:
         1. ensure_branch owner=username name=personal
            parent=system/main (no code copy)
         2. seed_stdlib with store.branch_id = system/main
            (additive persist — see seed-policy)
         3. checkout personal + rehydrate env from CTE
         4. refresh ui/nav shell

         load-library on CURRENT branch still upserts that
         branch (intentional pin). On personal/dev that pins
         the whole package — prefer small overrides or work
         name-by-name via define.

         (checkout "system/main") / (checkout "admin/personal")
         rehydrate sync; header shows current branch.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection shared-data-spaces
   (doc "Multi-identity collaboration on the data plane: named, membership-gated shared compartments data.s.<space>.* — NOT org-level branches")
   (status partial)
   (implements migrations/029_data_spaces.sql migrations/030_open_spaces.sql image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/prims_db.ml image/lib/tree_store.ml image/lib/http_data.ml image/test/data_spaces_test.ml libs/chat/lib.pp)
   (* agent note (|2026-09-06 OPEN SPACES + FIRST CONSUMER (branch chat-lobby-repair). DECISION: open spaces — data_spaces.open (migration 030) relaxes 'members only' to 'every authenticated identity is implicitly a member'; 'anonymous' is excluded on BOTH mirrors (SQL tree_path_in_member_space + OCaml is_space_member — keep them identical; a third mirror would be a bug farm). The lobby case (a space all users belong to without signup-churn membership management) motivated it; explicit spaces retain strict membership. FIRST CONSUMER: libs/chat lobby repair (multi-user.chat flip back to implemented, same date) — open 'chat-lobby' space, chat-room + chat-message admitted, lazy idempotent ensure in the package's say path. Verified live two-user on dev (both see both, data.s path proof, cross-identity data-history). MAKE HAZARDS (pay once, record for the next migration author): (1) BOOL WIRE FORMAT: pgx to_bool accepts ONLY the PostgreSQL bool wire repr 't'/'f' — a SELECT of EXISTS(...)::text yields 'true'/'false' and raises Conversion_failure IN THE RUNNING IMAGE while the identical query's unit tests pass (do not ask why the harness forgives; the invariant is: never ::text a boolean destined for driver decode — return the bool bare). (2) MIGRATION REPLAY CLOBBER: dev.sh/image-boot applies ALL migrations in sorted order on every start; an EARLIER migration that CREATE OR REPLACEs an object you later revised (023's tree_datum_path is IMMUTABLE and space-unaware) silently reverts your object whenever a checkout lacking your migration runs a start. Stood upright by re-applying 029 manually; the systemic note is that function-shape authority is LAST-SORTED-MIGRATION-WINS, and any checkout missing later migrations rewinds object shapes it replays. (3) Stranded-row reality matched spec: messages posted while the space-unaware function was in force stayed per-user and remained so post-repair (admission is future-writes).|) *)
   (* agent note (|2026-09-04 S3 MAKE DONE (branch s3-data-history): the LOG CONSUMER landed — Crud.data_history_json / data_history + (data-history 'type id) prim + /data row-page History card, reading Tree_store.versioned_history for the row's current path resolved through the visibility-confined view (THE KEY DESIGN POINT of this slice: storage was already free — the S3 test asserts against tree_versions reality that every S1/S2 write path (insert_datum, update_datum through crud prim + guarded variants) already shadows datum before-images with author stamps). Confined like get: ~viewer threads the actor (space rows check CALLER membership, author-runs-as-author bypassed). Output shape per spec: [{gen, author, created_at, delete, body}] newest-first, delete=true marks tombstone versions. /data row page gains a 'home:' meta line (full datum path — the compartment is visible at a glance) + the History card (gen · author · created_at · pretty body, deleted versions flagged). primitives docstring + db_cost Linear. VERIFIED: data_spaces_test 8/8 (S3: alice create -> alice update -> bob update on a space row yields 3 versions newest-first bob/alice/alice with bob's head body; carol's data-history is VNil). Full dune runtest exit 0; borge check/drift at baseline. STATUS planned->PARTIAL: all three make slices (S1/S2/S3) landed and the S1-S3 verify lines are live-green; PARTIAL not implemented because the details block's own remaining deferrals stand: the un-admit migrator (stranded-row recompute semantics), row-granular sharing ('share this one note' — v2), LIVE-ROWS-ONLY history (a deleted row's path no longer resolves through the view, so its history + tombstone is unreachable from data-history until a history-by-id surface — recorded in the crud.ml doc block), and the chat/user-plan repair (multi-user.borg stanzas stay partial until chat types are admitted to a lobby space as the first consumer).|) *)
   (* agent note (|2026-09-04 S2 MAKE DONE (branch s2-data-spaces): the prim surface landed. PRIMS (prims_db.ml, beside the data-lens block): space-create (any non-anon; creator joins as role='owner'), space-add-member / space-remove-member / space-admit (space owner — owner column OR role='owner' member — or kernel admin, resolved via Auth_store like require_admin), space-list (membership-scoped; kernel admin sees all), space-members (member-only; returns "user (role)"). THE GUARD: Crud.guard_space_write sits in the create/update/update_field/delete funnel — check (1) writing a space-ADMITTED type requires membership ("type X is admitted to space S; U is not a member", User_error raise, never a silent per-user write — the spec's load-bearing line), check (2) touching an existing space ROW requires membership in the row's space (path-derived via space_of_datum, so un-admitted types' stranded space rows stay gated). API SHAPE (revised mid-make after an optional-arg design hit warning-16-unerasable and a live dirty-tree caller blocked the merge): identity-driven surfaces thread the caller through Crud.update_guarded / update_field_guarded / delete_guarded (prims_db actor(), http_data Session.username); the master-signature Crud.update / update_field / delete are thin wrappers over the same _impl bodies with caller "unknown" — internal bookkeeping callers (ops_incidents et al.) keep compiling untouched, and any of them touching an admitted type errors loudly (the intended straggler behavior; thread the guarded variant to pass). create needs no variant — it already carried ~author, used as the guard caller. Routing itself was free (S1's tree_datum_path consults data_space_types at write time). NODE-PATH PARITY: Crud.node_path now consults data_space_types and Tree_store.datum_path gained ?space — the pure-mirror blind spot S1 recorded is closed for this consumer; other pure callers must look up admission themselves (documented on the tree_store doc). HYGIENE: primitives docstrings + db_cost tiers for the six new prims. VERIFIED: image/test/data_spaces_test.ml 7/7 on dev PG — S1 group unchanged + S2 group (space lifecycle incl. owner-gate denial + duplicate-create denial; admit routes member create to data.s + node-path parity + carol create/update/delete all raise + member update works + REMOVED member's create raises). Full dune runtest exit 0; borge check/drift at baseline. STATUS STAYS PLANNED: S3 (data-history consumer over tree_versions) remains; the un-admit migrator (stranded-row recompute) and row-granular sharing stay deferred as specced.|) *)
   (* agent note (|2026-09-04 S1 MAKE DONE (branch s1-data-spaces): the storage floor landed — migrations/029_data_spaces.sql (renumbered twice as parallel work claimed 027 (hash_domain) and 028 (branch_def_gen) mid-pass; migration numbers are claimed on MASTER, not on branches) creates data_spaces / data_space_members / data_space_types + tree_path_in_member_space(p,u) (STABLE — the one membership predicate, mirrors tree_is_seed_type's role for shared); tree_datum_path keeps its 4-arg signature and consults data_space_types (lookup order seed -> lens -> space -> user; a lens copy of an admitted type stays user-keyed — the space is the LIVE home, the lens is a per-user versioned copy), so ALL existing writers route with zero call-site change (insert_datum et al. delegate to the SQL function); tree_data_rows view + datum_user_isolation RLS gain the member-space disjunct (USING + WITH CHECK — members write space paths, non-members are rejected) and the view EXPOSES path (appended column) so crud.ml's visible_sql mirror applies the identical predicate prim-side. VERIFIED: image/test/data_spaces_test.ml 5/5 on dev PG (admitted routes to data.s / non-admitted stays data.u / view: two distinct member identities see, non-member doesn't / visible_sql mirror semantics / RLS floor under an unprivileged NOBYPASSRLS role incl. WITH CHECK write denial); full dune runtest exit 0; borge drift unchanged. Caller-membership (not author-runs-as-author) confirmed for the space compartment in visible_sql. S1 EDGES recorded in the migration header: un-admit path-recompute semantics (mirrors recompute to per-user on next write-touch), the OCaml pure datum_path mirror can't see admissions (node-path purity), no space column on data_rows — all S2-owned. STATUS STAYS PLANNED: S2 (space-create/add-member/space-admit prims + routing) and S3 (data-history consumer) remain; the S1 test lines in verify are now satisfiable on demand, S2/S3 lines stand.|) *)
   (* agent note (|2026-09-04 design discussion + plan-phase spec: USE CASE — a human collaborates with AGENT identities (separate users, 008_agent_users) on shared projects: a shared todo list both sides can read and write. Today data visibility is USER-scoped (one-tree.branching-interaction /data DECISION: data.u.<user>.* ∪ data.shared.*), so neither side sees the other's rows. THE REJECTED CANDIDATE: org-level branches. Branches are for CODE, not data this chapter's framing decision; one-tree /data): a branch costs exactly ZERO for data because fork/merge/promote structurally never touch a datum — so an org branch buys nothing for collaboration and drags in checkout / inheritance / merge / base_gen semantics the data plane explicitly does not have. THE REAL PRECEDENT is in-repo: migration 014 promoted guestbook_entry into data.shared.* when its per-user rows turned out invisible to other sessions. Spaces GENERALIZE that move from a global hardcoded allowlist to a named, membership-gated compartment. THE REJECTED ALTERNATIVE (local-first purist): no shared compartment; each identity keeps data.u.<identity>.* and collaboration is merge proposals across user subtrees using the shipped sync/push machinery. Rejected on the facts for this use case: (1) the collaborating agents are ONLINE and server-resident (POST /api/prim and /api/eval) — local-first's machinery exists for OFFLINE clients with no shared server, and its open research (sync-conflicts resolution policy) is not a price worth paying to avoid an RLS predicate; (2) the use case is a LIVE shared view ('see what agents are doing'), which federation has no answer for until merge time. THE ZERO-BUILD OPTION (agents auth AS the human: everything lands in data.u.admin.*) is recorded and rejected for >1 agent: authorship attribution collapses (every row says admin) and the agent-promote human-in-the-loop gate is lost. THE LOG QUESTION ('does the model keep a correct log'): attribution is free (data_rows.author = session username on every write, already), and tree-time's tree_versions (018-021) versions kind=datum on every destructive write, path-based, so data.s.* rows get before-images with no new machinery; what's missing is only the consumer — S3 builds (data-history). Includes the chat/plan repair: multi-user.borg's social layer assumed 'shared data_rows + handler filters', a mechanism migration 013 killed — spaces are its named home (see multi-user.borg chat/user-plan reconciles, same date).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PATH GRAMMAR (third compartment, additive):
           per-user  data.u.<user>.<type>.<id>       owner, single-head
           seed      data.shared.<type>.<id>         all sessions,
                                                     read-mostly
           space     data.s.<space>.<type>.<id>      members only,
                                                     multi-writer
           lens      data.l.<lens>.<user>.<type>.<id> UNCHANGED — the
                                                     opt-in versioning
                                                     axis, NOT a
                                                     collaboration unit

         MEMBERSHIP (one new table, additive migration):
           data_spaces(
             name       text PRIMARY KEY,
             owner      text NOT NULL,
             created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now())
           data_space_members(
             space    text NOT NULL REFERENCES data_spaces(name),
             username text NOT NULL,
             role     text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'member', -- 'member'|'owner'
             PRIMARY KEY (space, username))
         The owner manages members; members read + write. An identity
         (human or agent — no distinction; both are users) is in the
         visibility set iff it rows in data_space_members. Kernel admin
         with pp.user unset keeps its all-paths view (scripts / repair),
         unchanged from 013.

         VISIBILITY RULE (extends the 013 pattern, one predicate):
           A session sees exactly:
             data.u.<whoami>.*
             ∪ data.shared.*
             ∪ { data.s.<space>.* | whoami ∈ data_space_members(space) }
         Mechanism: a tree_seed_types()-style SQL helper
         tree_is_space_member(space, username) (or an EXISTS against
         data_space_members); tree_data_rows view gains the third
         disjunct; the datum_user_isolation RLS policy gains the same;
         crud.ml's (author = $u OR tree_is_seed_type(t)) confinement
         gains OR tree_path_in_member_space(path, $u). SET pp.user
         stays the request-scoped identity (prim-seam.server-endpoint:
         a proxied /api/prim call inherits exactly this visibility,
         because identity is server-asserted per request).

         ROUTING (which writes land in a space) — TYPE-LEVEL v1:
           (space-create "garden-agents")            owner = whoami
           (space-add-member "garden-agents" "agent-1") ; owner only
           (space-remove-member …) / (space-list) / (space-members s)
           (space-admit 'todo "garden-agents")
         A type ADMITTED INTO A SPACE lives there: (create 'todo …)
         routes by TYPE → tree_datum_path gains the space variant
         (data.s.<space>.<type>.<id>) exactly as 023 added the ~lens
         variant; get/rows/update/delete/find-row resolve the type's
         home and confine by membership. Type-level (not row-level)
         is the deliberate v1 cut: a todo LIST is either shared or
         personal; row-granular sharing ('share this one note') is
         honest v2 and must not be paid for now. A type lives in at
         most ONE space; un-admit moves the type home to per-user
         for FUTURE writes and LEAVES existing space rows in place
         (a migrator is v2; spaces are single-head, so stranded rows
         stay readable by members).

         WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY IS NOT:
           - NOT a branch. No fork, no merge, no inheritance chain,
             no checkout, no base_gen, no dirty/diverged status. The
             entire code-axis machinery is absent because it solves a
             problem the data plane does not have.
           - NOT versioned. Single-head rows, last-write-wins at row
             granularity, serialized by the one image (SERIALIZABLE,
             Store.with_transaction) — multi-writer on the server is
             a NON-EVENT. The versioning axis remains opt-in data.l.*;
             the offline-conflict axis remains local-first.sync-conflicts
             (and its policy menu now names space rows in scope for
             LAPTOP-LENS divergence only — see below).
           - NOT a sizing-up of data.shared. data.shared stays the
             global seed/wall corpus it is (world-readable on the
             habitat); spaces are the membership-gated middle.

         LOCAL-FIRST COUPLING (the one place spaces touch the sync
         model): a space subtree is multi-writer, so it can never be
         in any client lens's single-writer scope. It replicates
         under EXACTLY the data.shared policy: pulled, server-
         authoritative; a laptop lens's divergent edits to a space
         row are surfaced at sync (never silently resolved; server
         copy kept), applied via merge-request. Server-resident
         identities (the agents this subsection exists for) write
         spaces DIRECTLY — no reconciliation exists because no
         divergence exists. sync/push stays data.u.*-only until this
         lands in the replication unit (local-first.replication-units
         pinned block amended same date).

         AUTHOR-RUNS-AS-AUTHOR INTERACTION (decided): multi-user.borg's
         permissions stanza executes a function with ITS AUTHOR's data
         perms. For space rows that rule is BYPASSED: space visibility
         checks the CALLER's membership, or every agent could read the
         space through any admin-authored handler and, symmetrically, a
         removed member could still read the space via another member's
         handler. Caller-membership is the check; the author field
         stays attribution, not authority. (Personal data.u.* rows
         keep author-runs-as-author unchanged; only the space
         compartment re-keys on caller.)

         THE LOG (answers the design-discussion worry 'does it keep a
         correct log'): attribution is FREE — data_rows.author is the
         session username on every write, today. History is BUILT —
         tree-time's tree_versions (018-021) versions kind=datum on
         every destructive write, keyed by path: data.s.* rows get
         before-images per write with zero new storage. What is
         missing is only the CONSUMER: S3 adds
           (data-history 'todo <id>) -> VList version dicts
             newest-first {author, gen, body-as-json}
         over tree_versions datum rows for the path (any compartment),
         mirroring code-history's re-implementation over the same
         substrate; the /data browser drills into it.

         CONCURRENCY HONESTY: two members editing the SAME row
         simultaneously is row-level LWW under SERIALIZABLE — same
         semantics as data.shared today, with the lost update visible
         afterward via data-history (surfaced, never silent). Field-
         level merge / semantic merge per type is sync-conflicts'
         policy menu, not this subsection's.
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "S1: migration adds data_spaces + data_space_members; tree_datum_path gains the space variant; tree_data_rows + datum_user_isolation RLS gain the member-space disjunct; two member users see each other's space rows; a non-member's identical query returns nothing (RLS-enforced, not just view-filtered)")
    (test "S2: (space-admit 'todo s) routes (create 'todo …) to data.s.s.todo.<id>; (rows 'todo) as a member returns the union of members' space rows with per-row author attribution; a removed member's subsequent create errors with a membership error, not a silent per-user write")
    (smoke "S3: agent edits a shared todo; (data-history 'todo id) returns the human's before-image newest-first with author; the /data browser shows the space compartment and drills into history")
    (smoke "a proxied /api/prim (rows 'todo) under an agent's basic auth sees exactly the spaces that agent is a member of — no more"))
  )

  (subsection unbind
   (doc "Remove a branch-local binding; reveal shadowed system/main, never delete through")
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/primitives.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-05: implemented (unbind name) in Store.delete_function + session.ml prim. Tested on local dev: unbind branch-local override reveals system/main binding; unbind missing returns "nothing to unbind"; unbind on system/main errors. 160 dune tests green.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build")
    (test "dune runtest — unbind cases")
    (test "(unbind name) on a branch-local define removes the branch row; system/main binding revealed")
    (test "(unbind name) on system/main errors (cannot unbind on main)")
    (test "(unbind name) with no branch-local row errors (nothing to unbind)")
    (test "unbind never deletes another branch or system/main rows"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM: transient defines accumulate as permanent litter —
         agent probes, test fns, spike code. Every (define) on a
         personal branch is a permanent row. There is no way to
         remove a branch-local binding without deleting the whole
         branch.

         PRIM: (unbind name)
           Deletes ONLY the current branch's function_defs row for
           name. After unbind, name resolves via inheritance to
           whatever system/main (or a parent branch) provides —
           revealing the shadowed binding, if any.

         SEMANTICS:
           - On <user>/personal or <user>/dev: deletes the
             (branch_id, name) row from function_defs. The env
             rehydrates so the name now resolves to the inherited
             (system/main) binding, or becomes unbound if main
             has no such name.
           - On system/main: ERROR. Unbind never deletes a
             system/main row. (system/main is the inheritance
             root; removing from it would affect every user.)
           - If no branch-local row exists for name: ERROR
             (nothing to unbind). This prevents accidental no-ops
             and makes the caller explicit.
           - Never deletes rows from other branches.
           - Routes (view_bindings) are out of scope for v1;
             unbind is function_defs only.

         INTERACTION WITH PACKAGE PINS:
           load-library upserts/pins package bindings onto the
           current branch. (unbind name) on a pinned binding is
           an unpin — it removes the branch-local override,
           revealing the system/main package binding. A future
           (unbind-package pkg) could drop a whole pin; out of
           scope for v1.

         AUTH: admin or the branch owner (consistent with checkout
         / promote gating). Anonymous sessions cannot unbind.

         NON-GOALS:
           - Unbinding routes (view_bindings) — follow-up.
           - (unbind-package pkg) bulk unpin — follow-up.
           - Unbind on system/main (always errors).
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection pin-hygiene
   (doc "No diff, no pin: define-time suppression and the (prune-synced) sweep for contentless function pins")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-12-30: implemented same-session as the plan pass. Store.inherited_fn_body_matches (parent-chain closest-wins, canonical jsonb::text equality — the single hash domain branch-status recorded) gates persist_function: equal-to-inherited writes nothing and UNPINS an existing own row (system/main never matches, empty parent chain; boot seed via persist_function_if_absent bypasses). Store.prune_synced_fn_pins + (prune-synced) prim sweep synced fn pins (unbind semantics; parent revealed), guarded like unbind (anonymous denied, branch-owner rule, system/main no-op). Deletions batched in ONE SERIALIZABLE tx (deferred triggers flush once at commit) — swept 1.2k pins on prod in one request; per-pin autocommit ran ~0.7s/pin under load. branch_status_test: test_identical_pin_is_synced (which asserted the OLD pin-identical-bodies behavior) replaced by test_define_suppression (equal define no pin / divergent pins / resync unpins) + test_prune_synced (caught-up pin synced → pruned to clean; diff pin kept ahead); lifecycle test's synced-via-promote path still exercises the synced class. Full dune runtest green.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "define equal to the inherited body leaves no pin (status stays clean)")
    (test "re-defining a divergent pin back to the parent body unpins it; parent revealed")
    (test "system/main never suppresses (root author owns defaults); if-absent seed bypasses")
    (test "(prune-synced) removes exactly synced fn pins; ahead/ahead-new/diverged untouched")
    (test "synced pins still arise legitimately post-promote and classify synced"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         RULE: a PIN is a statement of intent (a diff), not a side
         effect of touching a name. branch-status made status
         content-based on the READ side (synced = own write,
         identical to parent), but the WRITE side stayed boolean:
         every (define) wrote a row whether or not it differed from
         what the branch would inherit. Observed on prod
         (2026-12-30): admin/personal carried 2,469 synced pins and
         admin/dev 466 — pure sludge from the copy-on-fork era,
         personal load-library upserts, and post-promote residue.
         Pin-hygiene closes the write side.

         1. WRITE-TIME SUPPRESSION (define / defmacro persist,
            install-function, ai-authoring Store.persist_function
            callers — all of them). Before Tree_store.upsert_fn,
            walk the parent chain (the branch itself excluded),
            closest-wins, canonical jsonb::text equality:
              - inherited body identical -> do NOT write; if an own
                row exists, UNPIN it (delete_function semantics),
                revealing the parent binding.
              - otherwise write as today.
            system/main never matches (empty parent chain): the
            root author owns defaults. persist_function_if_absent
            (boot seed) bypasses the check. Docstrings do not
            participate in identity — a suppressed define drops its
            docstring.

            Intended consequence: load-library on personal no longer
            pins the package — upserts identical to main leave no
            rows. Package pins exist only where the branch body
            genuinely differs. The AGENTS.md warning pivots from
            "load-library pins everything" to "load-library pins a
            package only insofar as it differs from main".

         2. (prune-synced) SWEEP for historical sludge and for pins
            that BECAME synced after the fact (the parent caught up
            to the pinned value; post-promote). Uses branch-status's
            synced class as its candidate set, delete_function as
            its mechanism (unbind; the parent value is revealed,
            nothing is lost):
              (prune-synced) -> VStr JSON {"pruned": N, "names": [...]}
            fn pins only (same scope as unbind v1: routes and types
            keep manual status-table review). Denied for anonymous;
            same branch-owner rule as unbind; no-op on system/main.

         SEMANTIC CAVEAT (accepted): an unbound name TRACKS the
         parent; a synced pin would have FROZEN the parent's value
         at pin time if the parent later moved. Suppression and
         prune therefore change the future behavior of contentless
         pins from "freeze" to "track". That is the point: intent-
         free rows must not freeze anything; a deliberate freeze
         carries an actual diff and classifies ahead/diverged.

         WHAT SURVIVES / RELATION TO branch-status: the synced class
         itself is NOT retired — pins can still legitimately be
         synced (post-promote: promote_functions upserts the target
         directly, leaving the source pin identical to the new
         parent; historical rows predating suppression). The status
         table, /branches dirty+synced columns, and the unbind hint
         remain correct for any pins created before/between
         enforcers; prune is the periodic broom, suppression is the
         door. Data rows are untouched (branch-free by the one-tree
         /data decision; data versioning is the opt-in data.l lens,
         a separate axis).

         4. LOAD-LIBRARY SKIP MAP (perf, 2026-09-08 — NOT pin
            semantics): load-library / load-string / reseed re-
            evaluate and re-persist every define in the release file;
            a big package (notes ~1000 defs) burned minutes re-
            evaluating unchanged bodies plus a parent-chain CTE per
            def for persist_function's no-diff-no-pin check. Before
            eval, load-library fingerprints the CHAIN-VISIBLE
            effective rows (list_function_names_for_load, ancestors-
            first replace-fold → closest non-hidden row wins — the
            same rows load_defs binds; fingerprint = pp body + doc)
            into the session skip map; eval_form skips a
            define/defmacro on an exact hit (returns VNil). What
            changed vs the original own-rows-only map: an UNPINNED
            INHERITED name with an identical lib body now skips too —
            the env already binds the inherited body and persist
            would be a no-op delete of a nonexistent row, so both
            were pure waste. Pin semantics unchanged: an own pin with
            a DIFFERENT body is the effective row and still misses
            the map (the unpin stays reachable); disabled-package and
            kernel-prim names stay out of the map (full eval path
            preserved). The skip is purely a perf window — it never
            creates or deletes rows, only avoids touching the store
            when the result would be a no-op.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection package-updates
   (doc "Authority model: live Postgres Nopales, git kernel + vault, additive seed")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: dual-era authority is operational — seed-policy additive, pp_sync vault, promote path, personal pins survive deploy. Matches AGENTS.md.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/session.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/loader.ml cli/bin/pp_sync.ml common/lib/branch.ml common/lib/manifest.ml common/lib/protocol.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         DUAL-ERA AUTHORITY (intentional, long-lived)
         ==========================================================
         Destination: stable OCaml core; most product change is
         live define/promote on the habitat. Until the core is
         calm, both surfaces exist — but each has one job.

         THREE CHANNELS:

         1. KERNEL — OCaml binary (HTTP, prims, loader, chrome).
            Source of truth: git. Update: push → build → restart.
            No branch involved.

         2. PACKAGE DEFAULTS — function_defs on system/main.
            Source of truth while running: Postgres system/main.
            Daily update path: promote from personal/dev
            (see promote). Boot seed is ADDITIVE only
            (see seed-policy): fills missing names from
            libs/*.pp; never overwrites an existing main row.
            Explicit reseed is the only git→main clobber.

         3. GARDEN / WORKSHOP PINS — rows on personal, dev, or
            other children. Never written by boot seed.
            Survive every deploy. Shadow main until unoverride
            or promote+unoverride.

         GIT'S TWO JOBS (do not mix):
           A. Kernel repo — authoritative for OCaml/migrations.
           B. Nopales vault — exported snapshots of packages/
              branches (backup, bootstrap, clone-to-laptop).
              Not the daily Nopales editor. See lisp-snapshot.

         DATA (data_rows) is never versioned by branch and
         never wiped by deploy. New package code must tolerate
         older row shapes or ship an explicit data migrator.

         DEFAULT WORK LOOP (product Nopales):
           work on personal or dev on the ONE habitat (prod)
           → dogfood against real shared data
           → promote selected names → system/main
           → optional snapshot/export into repo (vault)
           → optional unoverride so inheritance is clean
           Local dev.sh PG is kernel-lab only, not daily Nopales.

         UPDATE RULE (after additive seed):
           new name in libs, absent on main → appears on seed
           existing main name              → unchanged by seed
           personal/dev override           → still shadows main
           want git body on main           → explicit reseed
           want garden body on main        → promote

         CONSCIOUS UPDATE UX (branch browser; partial today):
           per name: Use main (unoverride) | Keep | Diff
           per package: Reset garden pins to main (bulk unoverride)
           load-library on non-main: warn that it pins the package

         Anti-patterns:
           - boot seed overwriting existing main defs
           - reseed onto personal/dev
           - auto-merge main→personal on boot
           - full copy-on-fork for personal (freezes forever)
           - treat data_rows as package payload
           - editing libs/*.pp as the primary product path
             while also defining the same names live without
             promote (split brain)
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build image cli")
    (smoke "boot seed does not change body of existing system/main fn")
    (smoke "boot seed inserts a libs name missing from system/main")
    (smoke "personal define survives image restart without pp_sync"))
  )
  (subsection seed-policy
   (doc "Additive boot seed into system/main; explicit reseed may clobber")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/session.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/loader.ml image/lib/primitives.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM TODAY:
         persist_function is INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE.
         seed_stdlib load-library therefore CLOBBERS every
         system/main name present in libs/*.pp on every boot.
         Live promote to main is not durable across deploy.

         OPTION A (chosen):

         1. ADDITIVE BOOT SEED
            seed_stdlib still runs on boot, still targets only
            system/main, still reads PP_LIBS_DIR/<pkg>/lib.pp.
            Persist path used by seed MUST NOT overwrite:
              INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (branch_id, name) DO NOTHING
            or equivalent "insert if absent" helper
            (Store.persist_function_if_absent / seed mode flag).
            Missing package files: skip + log (unchanged).
            Routes/deftype seeded from libs: same additive rule
            where a natural key exists (branch_id, route|name).

         2. EXPLICIT RESEED (git → main clobber, admin only)
            Prim: (reseed-package "notes")
              optional (reseed-package "notes" 'force) same.
            Behavior:
              - require admin (same gate as promote to system/*)
              - read libs/<pkg>/lib.pp via loader
              - eval/persist onto system/main with UPSERT
                (full overwrite for names in that file)
              - does not touch personal/dev rows
              - rehydrate callers as needed
            CLI: pp-sync reseed <pkg>  (calls the prim)
            Optional later: pp-sync reseed --all

         3. load-library / load-string (interactive)
            Unchanged intent: write the CURRENT branch with
            upsert. Still the pin hammer. Not used by boot
            seed once seed has its own absent-only persist.
            Warn when current branch is not system/main
            (package-updates UX; may land with change-set).

         4. FRESH DATABASE
            Additive seed on empty main = full bootstrap from
            libs/*.pp. No separate first-boot mode required.

         5. NON-GOALS
            - seed writing personal/dev
            - auto reseed on git push without admin prim
            - deleting main names absent from libs
              (additive never removes; cleanup is manual
               or a future prune tool)
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build image cli")
    (smoke "after promote name to main, restart leaves body intact")
    (smoke "(reseed-package pkg) admin overwrites main from libs")
    (smoke "non-admin reseed-package errors")
    (smoke "reseed-package does not write personal rows"))
  )
  (subsection vault-authority
   (doc "The vault is a generated MIRROR of system/main, not an authoring source; drift is visible")
   (status implemented)
   (implements cli/bin/pp_sync.ml cli/lib/pp_sync_diff.ml cli/lib/pp_sync_merge.ml scripts/post-receive-prod-hook.ksh)
   (details
    (doc (|
         DECISION (roerick + pi, 2026-10-02, card 8dff7c9e):
         Postgres system/main stays AUTHORITATIVE for package Nopales.
         libs/<pkg>/lib.pp is a GENERATED MIRROR (vault) — a backup,
         bootstrap, and clone-to-laptop snapshot. It is NOT hand-edited
         as the product path.

         WHY THIS NEEDS SAYING: the additive boot seed (seed-policy)
         only ADDS names absent on main; it deliberately never updates
         an existing body. So a fix authored in libs/ for an existing
         define is committed but NEVER DEPLOYED — the 2026-10-02
         incident: 435 live-only defines across 21 packages, prod
         notes/doc/upsert-in still returning its pre-fix body while git
         HEAD raised. The vault had drifted into looking like an
         authoring surface while behaving like a read-only snapshot.

         THE TWO PROBLEMS ARE DISTINCT and must not be conflated:
           (a) STALE VAULT — libs/ != live main (names AND bodies).
           (b) NO DEPLOY PATH — existing names never update on deploy.
         The only modes were additive (won't update) and reseed (blind
         clobber of every live-only define). There is no safe
         "update this name from the vault" mode, and this decision does
         not add one; it removes the expectation that one is needed.

         CONSEQUENCES (the working rules):
           1. Product Nopales changes go live on the HABITAT (personal/
              dev) then PROMOTE to system/main. That is the deploy.
           2. The vault is refreshed by pp-sync snapshot (live -> git),
              scheduled/CI, never treated as source. pp-sync pull is a
              capture verb, not an editor.
           3. Kernel changes (OCaml, migrations) still ship normally
              through the release archive.
           4. Never run reseed on a drifted package to "land" a fix —
              that is the silent live-only clobber (the reseed guard
              refuses without --force).

         DRIFT VIEW (card 8dff7c9e item 1): pp-sync status compares
         canonical AST BODIES, not just names, in BOTH directions.
           - only-local / only-live: name-set drift (was already there)
           - body-stale: a name present both places whose LIVE body
             differs from the vault body — the exact git-has-the-fix /
             main-does-not signal. --verbose prints the pair as a diff
             (canonical expr pretty-printed, one line each side).
           - --names-only is the fast blind pass (name set only).
         Bodies compare via Ast.expr_to_body_json on BOTH the local
         file and the live export-package text, so equality is
         AST-level and whitespace/comment-insensitive; the docstring is
         metadata, excluded like the store excludes it.

         VERIFY-AFTER-DEPLOY: a body a release claims to ship is not
         live until pp-sync status reports body-stale: 0 for its
         packages. That is the honest check that the .pp half of a
         release deployed.

         EXPORTER MEMBERSHIP (i-vault-exporter, 2026-10-02): a snapshot
         mirrors live main by calling (export-package pkg), which finds
         a package's bindings. The OLD rule matched names by the
         `pkg/`-prefix, which is wrong for any package whose defines do
         not share its name — std defines list/…, html/…, css/…; each
         theme-asNNN defines theme/…; memory defines mem/…. For those,
         export-package returned an EMPTY body (export list only) and a
         snapshot wrote it over the vault: libs/std would have gone 69
         defines -> 0, and every theme-asNNN 35 -> 0. Membership now
         comes from function_defs.package PROVENANCE (the (package …)
         form's stamp, set at load), which records the declaring
         package regardless of name shape; the prefix match survives
         only as a fallback for unstamped legacy rows. Same fix
         strengthens (package-bindings pkg) and describe.

         SNAPSHOT GUARD (same task, superseded 2026-10-02 by dropped-name
         accounting but kept here for history): live main does not
         contain every vault package. On-demand skins (theme-asNNN) are
         deliberately never seeded (boot avoids thrashing in-memory
         theme/* before rehydrate), and hand-authored files
         (libs/memory) may define names no package form claims. The
         original count-refusal (fewer defines than on disk) became
         DROPPED-NAME ACCOUNTING (see THE ONE WRITE PATH): a name may
         leave a file only while live main still binds it SOMEWHERE.
         The original hazard (empty/broken export gutting
         std/theme-as 69→0) still refuses — every dropped name is
         unaccounted when the exporter is wrong.

         COMMENT-PRESERVING MERGE (i-vault-comments, 2026-10-02): the
         vault is LITERATE SOURCE — comment blocks explain non-obvious
         code and exist ONLY in git (the store keeps the AST;
         export-package emits no interior comments). Measured: filmstrip
         146 comment lines in git vs 2 in the live export; todo 110 vs
         ~0. A plain generator write would delete that documentation.
         So a snapshot MERGES new export into the existing file
         (cli/lib/pp_sync_merge.ml): every top-level form whose canonical
         AST is UNCHANGED is emitted VERBATIM (comments, formatting and
         order kept); only genuinely changed forms take the new text.
         Forms match by NAME, so reordering is harmless. A changed form
         keeps its old LEADING prefix (section comment) but its interior
         comments are replaced by the new body — that is REPORTED by name
         (changed_with_comments), never silent. Hand-authored files with
         ZERO live defines are still refused outright; --force bypasses
         merge+guards for a deliberate rewrite.

         THE ONE WRITE PATH (2026-10-02 review): pull, snapshot, and
         land all decide through ONE pure gate (Pp_sync_merge.plan_write,
         driven by cli vault_write) — before, snapshot was guarded but
         pull and land wrote RAW generator output (the blessed land
         path stripped comments itself; a pull over 20 packages survived
         only because they were comment-free). Gate order:
         new file → raw write; hand-authored + zero live defines →
         REFUSE; hand-authored + live content → MERGE keeping the old
         first prefix (the custom header — the three-way hand-authored
         distinction resolves by define count, not by header alone:
         std merges, theme-* skins refuse); DROPPED-NAME ACCOUNTING
         replaces the old count guard — a file may drop a name ONLY
         while live main still binds it SOMEWHERE (a cross-package
         re-homing like chat→ui of ui/bubble, or re-entry at root, is
         an honest mirror edit, reported as removed); a name bound
         NOWHERE on append-only main (no primitive deletes anything
         from system/main) is an anomaly and REFUSES by name; otherwise
         MERGE. --force is the reviewed override everywhere and warns
         about the comment lines a raw overwrite destroys. The
         accounting needs a full live-name set: snapshot unions every
         export + (package-bindings "") (root) + the session env —
         (list-bindings) alone is the lazily-bound session env, NOT
         the binding table.

         PREVIEW HONESTY: --dry-run runs every guard and merge against
         the same paths a real run would touch and writes nothing.
         `--out /tmp` previews bypassed every guard (no old file at
         the out path to compare) — they can't approve a run.

         STATUS TARGET: `pp-sync status` compares the vault against
         system/main BY DEFAULT (the mirror source) — it checks out,
         compares, restores. `--branch <ref>` is honored, labeled as
         garden drift so personal-branch noise cannot masquerade as
         vault drift (the 2026-10-02 hand-off's anomaly lists were
         partly computed against admin/personal).

         RESEED GUARD is two-sided (2026-10-02): live-only NAMES (the
         delete hazard) AND same-name BODY drift (the rollback
         hazard — reseed overwriting newer live bodies with stale
         vault text, silently, because every name exists on both
         sides). Natural recovery: pp-sync pull <pkg> (the guarded
         merge), review, reseed; --force is the deliberate override.

         BANNED FAMILIES (Pp_sync_vault.vault_banned): live main rows
         that must never materialize a vault file — `list`, std's old
         shape, deleted from libs/ twice (6ed8656 kernel-form seeding
         abort, 628416f a pull-all resurrected it once). Discovery
         skips banned families silently; explicit verbs refuse loudly.
         The ban is POLICY, not cleanliness: deleting a define row from
         system/main has no primitive yet (unbind refuses on the
         inheritance root); lift the ban only when a guarded
         main-delete primitive deletes the live duplicate rows.

         MID-RUN BRANCH-FLIP GUARD (2026-10-02 live incident): eval
         sessions are PER-IDENTITY and shared — a concurrent admin
         client can flip the session checkout between pp-sync's eval
         calls, silently redirecting the mirror (a real snapshot raced
         a parallel agent session and emitted empty shells stamped
         admin/personal mid-run). Every export text self-reports its
         provenance (the 'exported from live branch B' header);
         vault_write REFUSES any export whose reported branch differs
         from the expected target — loud skip, re-run when the other
         session is quiet (or give sync its own identity, the clean
         isolation). Zero-define exports (junk family shells) cannot
         create new vault files.
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build image cli")
    (smoke "pp-sync status reports body-stale when a live body differs from the vault")
    (smoke "pp-sync status --names-only skips the body diff")
    (smoke "pp-sync status body-stale: 0 after a snapshot pulls live bodies into the vault")
    (smoke "pp-sync pull refuses a hand-authored file with zero live defines")
    (smoke "pp-sync pull merges (does not strip) a hand-authored file with live content")
    (smoke "pp-sync snapshot/pull/land refuse a name main binds nowhere, by name")
    (smoke "pp-sync snapshot --dry-run prints the same report it would write")
    (smoke "pp-sync reseed refuses same-name body drift without --force"))
  )
  (subsection promote
   (doc "Graduate garden/workshop code to system/main; optional vault export")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/session.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/http_branches.ml cli/bin/pp_sync.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Promote is the blessed path for product Nopales.
         Git export is optional vault/checkpoint, not required
         for main to keep the change across deploys (seed-policy).

         A. LIVE MAIN (required for shared defaults)
            Selective upsert of named function_defs from a
            source branch (personal/dev/…) → system/main.
            Already: Session.promote_to_main, Store.promote_functions,
            POST /branches/promote (admin only into system/*).
            Also: (merge src dst) for coarser branch merges;
            promote remains the selective UI/prim path.

            ROUTES TRAVEL WITH THEIR HANDLER:
            promote MUST also upsert any view_bindings row on
            the source whose handler names a promoted function
            (preserving visibility). A promoted handler without
            its route leaves main serving the old binding —
            the split-brain footgun this rule exists to kill.
            Routes whose handler is NOT in the promote set do
            not travel. promote does not touch type_defs
            (schema-authority).

         B. VAULT EXPORT (optional durability in git)
            export-package / pp-sync pull writes libs/<pkg>/lib.pp
            (or snapshot tree — see lisp-snapshot). Operator
            commits. This does NOT change live main by itself.
            Useful for backup, laptop clone, and explicit reseed.

         HAPPY PATH:
           edit on personal or dev → dogfood
           → promote selected names → system/main
           → optional unoverride on source (clean inheritance)
           → optional pp-sync snapshot/pull + git commit (vault)

         SELECTIVE:
           Ship named functions (ui/nav, notes/save, …),
           not the entire garden by default.
           UI: name multi-select on branch detail (exists);
           package-group select may come later.

         PRIM / CLI (v1 make):
           keep promote UI + Session.promote_to_main
           optional Nopales: (promote-to-main "name") or
             (promote-to-main '("n1" "n2")) on current branch
           pp-sync does not promote (image policy stays in image)

         Policy:
           - promote/merge into system/*: admin only
           - merge into own branches: free
           - data_rows never part of promote
           - image never git-pushes (no repo credentials)
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "POST /branches/promote copies selected names to system/main")
    (smoke "promoted main body survives restart under additive seed")
    (smoke "non-admin promote to system/main denied")
    (smoke "promote of a handler fn also copies its view_bindings row")
    (smoke "view_bindings whose handler is not promoted stay put"))
  )
  (subsection code-history
   (doc "Append-only before-image history for code rows, captured by Postgres triggers")
   (status implemented)
   (implements migrations/012_code_history.sql image/lib/store.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM:
         Every write to function_defs / type_defs /
         view_bindings is a destructive INSERT ... ON CONFLICT
         DO UPDATE. Redefine a function and its old body is
         unrecoverable unless someone happened to pp-sync
         snapshot first. Merge and promote clobber the same
         way. There is no history anywhere in the system.

         DESIGN: HISTORY TABLES + TRIGGERS (capture below
         every write path)

           code_history(
             id         bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,
             rel        text,          -- 'function_defs' | 'type_defs' | 'view_bindings'
             branch_id  uuid,
             name       text,          -- fn/type name or route path
             body       jsonb,         -- superseded before-image (fn/type body)
             author     text,
             doc        text,
             package    text,
             op         text,          -- 'UPDATE' | 'DELETE'
             changed_at timestamptz DEFAULT now()
           )
           index on (rel, branch_id, name, changed_at DESC)

         One BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE FOR EACH ROW trigger per
         live table inserts OLD into code_history. Triggers,
         not OCaml call-site instrumentation, because every
         write path converges on these tables: define, merge,
         promote, reseed, boot seed, and even raw psql. A
         trigger cannot be bypassed by a new call site.

         TRANSACTIONALITY: the trigger runs in the same
         transaction as the write. A rolled-back merge leaves
         no history rows; a committed merge leaves one per
         clobbered row. History is therefore exact, never
         optimistic.

         SEMANTICS:
         - INSERT fires nothing: first definition has no
           before-image. Version N of a name = live row plus
           N-1 code_history entries walking changed_at DESC.
         - ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE fires the UPDATE trigger for
           conflicted rows only — merge/promote history is
           exactly the clobbered set, for free.
         - INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING (additive seed)
           writes nothing and records nothing.
         - op='DELETE' rows are how unoverride/branch-drop
           remain recoverable.

         NOPALES PRIMS (read-only):
           (code-history "name")            -- current branch
           (code-history "name" "owner/branch")
             → JSON list, newest first:
               {rel, op, author, doc, changed_at, body}
           (code-diff "name" n)             -- current row vs
             nth-newest history body (unified-ish text diff)
         Restore is NOT a prim in v1: read the old body and
         define it back (that define itself lands in history,
         so restore-by-redefine is lossless).

         SCOPE / NON-GOALS (v1):
         - CODE ROWS ONLY. data_rows history is a separate
           later decision (storage commitment; shared table).
         - No retention pruning; review table size later.
         - No history UI in v1 beyond the prims; the
           branch-browser Changes view may surface it later.
         - tree_nodes mirrors are derived state; history
           lives on the authoritative tables only.
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/history_test.ml")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "redefine a fn on personal; (code-history name) returns the old body")
    (smoke "merge that clobbers target rows records one history row per clobber")
    (smoke "additive boot seed records no history rows")
    (smoke "rolled-back transaction leaves no history rows"))
  )
  (subsection branch-resolved-reads
   (doc "Every code-read prim gains the optional owner/branch ref that code-history already has — function-source, function-ast, find-code, code-diff — plus a data footprint on branch-changes — so the operator inspects a fork without checking out of their own chain.")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/store_defs.ml image/lib/agent_tools.ml image/lib/prims_code.ml image/lib/prims_graph.ml image/test/branch_resolved_reads_test.ml)
   (* agent note (|2026-10-02: burn from the marcus codex recon — the only way to read another branch's pinned BODY today is checkout, and per-identity sessions make checkout re-point the WHOLE shared identity (the operator's browser included). code-history already takes owner/branch; code-diff and function-source do not, so fork bodies were literally unreadable without a global side effect. Also: branch-changes lists fn/route own rows but NOT datum rows, so a fork that only wrote data (a guest's notes/todo footprint) reads as EMPTY.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         WHY
         Per-identity sessions mean checkout is an IDENTITY-WIDE
         move: a shared admin session that checks out a guest
         garden repoints every caller of that identity until
         checked back. A global side effect is the wrong tool
         for a read. code-history already proved the safe
         shape (optional owner/branch, no checkout) — this
         subsection makes every code-read prim use it.

         WHAT GAINS THE REF
         - (function-source name [owner/branch])
         - (function-ast name [owner/branch])
         - (find-code needle [owner/branch])
         - (code-diff name n [owner/branch])
         - (branch-changes [owner/branch]) GAINS a data
           summary: typed datum rows authored on the branch
           (type, count, authors, newest) — today a fork that
           only wrote data shows as EMPTY and the operator
           must guess at type names and probe readers.
         Non-current-branch reads never populate the session
         env: source reads only, no eval, no env pinning.

         SCOPE GRAMMAR (a NORM owed after the same recon)
         Read prims must DECLARE their scope, because today
         they differ silently and it burns operator time:
         find-code is chain-visible; find-rows-author is
         whole-store under the admin RLS disjunct; find-text
         and tree/find-by are garden-scoped with a prefix
         escape. During the 2026-10-02 recon these three
         answers gave three different replies to "does
         marcus have data?". The (describe …) surface must
         carry a SCOPE line for every read prim, and a new
         reader may not introduce a fourth scoping kind
         without a line here.

         NON-GOALS
         - no cross-branch writes (fork/merge/promote own that)
         - no evaluation of foreign bodies (reads only)
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection land
   (doc "pp-sync land: promote + vault snapshot + git commit as one operator step")
   (status implemented)
   (implements cli/bin/pp_sync.ml scripts/pp-sync.sh cli/lib/pp_client.ml cli/lib/pp_sync_vault.ml scripts/verify_libs_layout.sh)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM:
         The blessed loop (work on personal/dev → promote →
         snapshot → git commit) has manual steps 3–4. Skip
         them and live main drifts from libs/ silently;
         pp-sync status can see the drift but nothing runs it.

         LAND — the one-command ship path:
           pp-sync land <pkg> [name ...] [--from owner/branch]
         performs, in order, from the operator's laptop:
           1. CHECKOUT-INDEPENDENT READ: export the named
              functions (or the whole package when no names
              given) from the source branch (default: the
              session's current branch).
           2. PROMOTE: call the promote prim for those names
              (admin gate unchanged; routes travel per
              promote).
           3. SNAPSHOT: re-export the same names from
              system/main and write libs/<pkg>/lib.pp.
           4. COMMIT: git add libs/<pkg>/lib.pp && git commit
              with message "vault(<pkg>): land <names>".
              The image never git-pushes and never holds repo
              credentials; land runs where the repo lives.
           5. REPORT: print promoted names, conflicts the
              promote overwrote (visible via code-history),
              and the commit SHA.

         Failure stops the pipeline at the failed step and
         reports state (nothing after a failed promote is
         snapshotted; a failed commit leaves main promoted
         and the vault dirty, printed clearly).

         DRIFT VISIBILITY:
           pp-sync status gains an exit code: 0 clean,
           1 drift. The repo monitor command (README) runs
           pp-sync status so drift shows up on the dashboard
           instead of being discovered by accident.

         NON-GOALS:
           - land does not merge branches (promote only)
           - land does not push to remotes
           - land does not unoverride the source branch
             (stays an explicit, separate act)
           - no long-running service; land is operator/agent-invoked
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build cli")
    (smoke "pp-sync land notes ui/nav promotes, snapshots libs/notes/lib.pp, commits")
    (smoke "failed promote (non-admin) leaves vault untouched and reports")
    (smoke "pp-sync status exits 1 when libs/ differs from live main"))
  )
  (subsection lisp-snapshot
   (doc "Clone habitat Nopales state into the git repo as vault/backup")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/session.ml image/lib/primitives.ml cli/bin/pp_sync.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PURPOSE:
         Postgres is the live Nopales store. The repo must still
         be able to hold a clone of package/branch state for
         backup, new-machine bootstrap material, and explicit
         reseed inputs — without becoming the daily editor.

         V1 SURFACES (compose existing export):

         1. Package pull (exists)
            (export-package "notes") → lib.pp text
            pp-sync pull <pkg>       → libs/<pkg>/lib.pp
            pp-sync pull-all         → every discovered pkg
            Default export reads the SESSION's current branch
            visibility (CTE). For vault of defaults, checkout
            system/main first OR pass an explicit branch
            (v1 make: add optional branch ref).

         2. Snapshot command (new CLI)
            pp-sync snapshot
              - checkout-aware: export all packages with
                bindings on the chosen branch
              - writes libs/<pkg>/lib.pp by default (main vault)
            pp-sync snapshot --branch system/main
            pp-sync snapshot --branch admin/personal \
              --out backups/personal/
              - garden pins land under backups/ not libs/
                so bootstrap seed does not treat pins as defaults

         3. Image prim (thin)
            (export-package pkg) already returns text.
            Optional: (snapshot-packages) → list of pkg names
            with own or visible bindings (helper for CLI).
            No git commit inside the image.

         4. Operator habit
            Before risky reseed or migration: snapshot main
            (+ personal if desired) and git commit.
            Cadence backups may be cron + pp-sync snapshot.

         LAYOUT CONVENTION:
           libs/<pkg>/lib.pp     bootstrap + main vault (seed input)
           backups/<ref>/...     non-main branch dumps (not seeded)

         NON-GOALS v1:
           - automatic git commit from image or CLI
           - pg_dump of data_rows as part of pp-sync
           - encrypting snapshots
           - bidirectional sync service
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build cli")
    (smoke "pp-sync pull notes writes libs/notes/lib.pp")
    (smoke "pp-sync snapshot --branch system/main refreshes libs")
    (smoke "pp-sync snapshot --branch u/personal --out backups/personal does not write libs"))
  )
  (subsection branch-browser-ui
   (doc "Git-like light /branches browser: list, open, stats, checkout, promote")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: http_branches.ml ~710 lines — list with own code/overrides, detail, checkout, fork, promote to main + soft lint. Spec "TODAY bare table" is stale.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/http.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         TODAY: GET /branches is a bare table
         (owner/name, parent uuid prefix, created).
         No checkout, stats, detail, or promote.

         GOAL: tiny Gitea-ish branch page in classic
         htmx chrome — browse, open, stats, checkout,
         later promote — no SPA.

         ROUTES:
           GET  /branches
           GET  /branches?ref=owner/name   detail
           POST /branches/checkout         ref=…
           POST /branches/fork             from + name
           POST /branches/merge            src + dst (later)
           POST /branches/promote          selective (later)

         LIST PAGE:
           Header strip: On: <current> ★ + quick links
           Table columns:
             ★ | branch | parent | own code |
             overrides | routes | age | actions
           own code = COUNT function_defs WHERE branch_id
             (no CTE). overrides = own names also on parent.
           actions: Open · Checkout (disabled if current)
           Labels: system/main "defaults"; */personal "garden"
           Create card: fork-from select + new name + Create
             (full fork for experiments; personal stays empty)

         DETAIL PAGE (?ref=):
           Title, parent link, Checkout, back to list
           Stats: own fns, overrides, own routes,
             visible fn count (CTE), last define, authors
           Sections (htmx partials):
             Overrides (default for personal) — name,
               author, updated, new|override, view, diff
             All own code
             Routes (own view_bindings)
             Activity / Changes: branching.change-set
               (ORDER BY updated_at on own defs + transcript)
           Diff vs parent v1: two <pre> columns of pp_expr
             (identical / changed / only-here / only-parent)

         CHECKOUT:
           POST → Session.checkout_to → 303 /branches
           or HX-Redirect. Defines already persisted; no
           "uncommitted" confirm.

         PROMOTE PANEL (detail, admin):
           package multi-select + name multi-select
           [ ] Merge into system/main
           [ ] Mark for export (pp_sync pull list)
           Never dump whole garden by default.

         UPDATES BADGE (package-updates):
           branching.branch-status supersedes the
           updated_at badge: status = behind | synced
           tells accept-vs-keep by CONTENT (behind =
           main moved, offer accept/unoverride;
           synced = identical pin, offer unbind).

         STORE HELPERS (own-branch, no CTE unless noted):
           count_functions_on_branch
           count_routes_on_branch
           list_own_function_names
           branch_status (branching.branch-status:
             clean/ahead-new/ahead/behind/diverged/
             synced — replaces the retired
             list_overrides name-presence join)
           lookup_function_on_branch (no CTE)
           delete_function_on_branch (unoverride)

         NON-GOALS (light):
           git SHAs/commits, PR review UI, blame,
           per-branch data, fancy fork-tree viz
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build")
    (smoke "GET /branches shows current ★ and checkout")
    (smoke "POST checkout switches current-branch")
    (smoke "detail lists personal overrides vs main")
    (smoke "detail Changes section lists own defs by updated_at"))
  )
  (subsection change-set
   (doc "Branch change set / transcript: what changed on this branch")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/http_branches.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "GET /changes?ref=u/personal lists own fns by updated_at")
    (smoke "(branch-changes) returns own function names + updated_at on current branch")
    (smoke "after define on personal, name appears at top of changes")
    (smoke "change row links to /edit and /bindings detail + parent diff when override"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         WHY (Smalltalk change set / method transcript)
         ==========================================================
         Combo pack item #2. After live defines, humans and agents
         need "what did I change on this garden?" without git.
         Not a full VCS — a living transcript of own-branch code
         rows + optional session ring of recent defines.

         Compounds with:
           - image.inspector impact-queue (same define hook)
           - image.system-browser detail → link here for overrides
           - branching.branch-browser-ui detail Activity section
           - promote / package-updates (know what is dirty)

         ==========================================================
         A. DERIVED CHANGE SET (no new table required v1)
         ==========================================================
         Source of truth already exists:
           Store.list_own_functions ~branch_id
             → (name, author, updated_at)
           Store.list_own_routes ~branch_id
           Store.branch_status ~branch_id
             → clean/ahead-new/ahead/behind/diverged/
               synced per name (branching.branch-status;
               supersedes the retired list_overrides
               new|override name-presence classes)

         "Changes" for branch B =
           own function_defs ∪ own view_bindings on B,
           sorted by updated_at DESC (activity order).
         Classify each fn row:
           - new: no parent row of same name
           - override: parent has name (optionally note if
             parent.updated_at > child → main-newer badge)
           - route: own view_bindings entries (path → handler)

         Nopales prims (read-only, current branch default):
           (branch-changes) → list of dict/JSON strings
             {name, kind: fn|route, author, updated_at,
              class: clean|ahead-new|ahead|behind|
                     diverged|synced}
             (branching.branch-status; was new|override|route —
              superseded 2026-08-28, content-based now)
           (branch-changes "owner/name") optional other branch
             if visible to user (same rules as /branches detail)

         ==========================================================
         B. SESSION TRANSCRIPT (define hook)
         ==========================================================
         Same write path as impact-queue (persist_define /
         install_function success):
           append {name, at, branch, author, op: define|route|delete}
           to session ring (last N=64). Optional; derived list
           from PG is enough if ring is empty after restart.

         Nopales: (change-transcript) → recent session ops
         Do not require durable data_rows log in v1.

         ==========================================================
         C. UI SURFACES
         ==========================================================
         1) Branch detail (primary):
              GET /branches?ref=owner/name&view=changes
              or default Activity section expanded for personal
              Table: when | name | class | author | open | diff
              open → /bindings?pkg=&fn= or /edit/<name>
              diff → existing ?diff= two-column pp_expr

         2) Optional GET /changes on current branch (short alias
            redirecting to /branches?ref=<current>&view=changes)

         3) system-browser function detail: if name is own-row,
            badge "on this branch" + link into Changes highlight

         ==========================================================
         D. AGENT
         ==========================================================
         After a multi-define session, agent can (branch-changes)
         or read /changes to summarize dirty garden before promote.
         install_function result may append "changeset: N own fns"
         lightly — impact text remains primary for smoke routes.

         ==========================================================
         E. NON-GOALS
         ==========================================================
           - Per-edit revision history table (later; updated_at only)
           - git commits / SHAs / blame
           - Diff of data_rows (code only)
           - Cross-user global firehose
           - Blocking define on "uncommitted" state

         ==========================================================
         F. PHASES
         ==========================================================
         C1: UI Activity/Changes from list_own_* + overrides class
         C2: (branch-changes) prim + /changes alias
         C3: session transcript ring on define (shared hook w/ impact)
         C4: browser detail deep-link + agent one-liner

         SUCCESS: on personal after editing ui/nav, Changes shows
         ui/nav at top as override; diff vs main works; agent can
         list dirty names without scraping HTML.
    |))
   )
  )
    (subsection checkpoints
     (doc "Named points on the gen stream: branch/cp, branch/cp-list, branch/cp-diff, branch/cp-restore + the branch/log oplog face")
      (status implemented)
     (* agent note (|2026-10-12 plan-phase ratification: roerick OK'd checkpoints + the log face in-conversation tonight ("checkpoints yes"). The substrate already records every write (tree_versions append-only, per-branch branch_generations counter); the only missing primitive is NAMING a point. jj names an implicitly-snapshotted working copy; DeltaDB sells addressable deltas — both reduce to names pinned to gens here. Spec written directly in plan phase, no issue filed.|) *)
     (* agent note (|2026-10-12 implemented same-session as the plan pass (roerick approved the overnight run): migration 040 branch_checkpoints (UNIQUE(branch_id,name), name CHECK [a-z0-9-]+, ON DELETE CASCADE), image/lib/store_checkpoints.ml as the 7th store family (store facade include + verify_store_split.sh updated; opaque jsonb only), prims branch/cp + branch/cp-list + branch/cp-diff + branch/cp-restore + branch/log in session_control_prims.ml (server-only, anonymous-denied, ref-readable like branch/status), effect.ml write rows for cp + cp-restore, image/test/checkpoints_test.ml 4/4 green + dune build @all. Judgment calls: (1) cp gen = the branch's OWN branch_generations counter captured atomically in one INSERT..SELECT (never the ancestry-walk max, so ancestor moves never skew the window); duplicate names refuse — immutable anchors, not moving tags. (2) cp-diff = the branch_status decision discipline windowed (hash equality with ABSENT as its own value): changed / added-since / removed-since; unchanged filtered. (3) cp-restore default set = changed + removed-since only — added-since pins have NO captured body to restore to and are skipped with a reason; restore lands NEW writes through the tree write helpers (version trail + reverse mirrors + code_history before-images; gen only moves forward; base_gen untouched; system/main refuses; env rehydrates when anything lands). (4) branch/log reads the version trail (every def write fires it); code_history is the recovery substrate restore consumes, not a second oplog source; windowed by cp = gen > cp.gen, else last 64 writes.|) *)
     (details
      (doc (|
           ==========================================================
           WHY
           ==========================================================
           "Value of path P on branch B at gen G" is already a
           READ (branch-status details; tree_versions is
           append-only, content-hashed, gen-stamped). code-history
           keeps every before-image per name. Nothing NAMES a
           point. An agent mid-make cannot say "back to before the
           lens rework" except by carrying gen numbers in its head;
           a reviewer cannot be handed a stable anchor for "the
           work as it stood at X". Checkpoints are names pinned to
           gens — the one primitive the jj/DeltaDB aesthetic names
           that the substrate does not.

           ==========================================================
           STORAGE (additive migration)
           ==========================================================
             branch_checkpoints(
               id          bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY
                             PRIMARY KEY,
               branch_id   uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES branches(id),
               name        text NOT NULL,   -- [a-z0-9-]+
               gen         bigint NOT NULL, -- branch head gen at cp time
               message     text,
               author      text NOT NULL,
               created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
               UNIQUE (branch_id, name)
             )

           GEN CAPTURE borrows branch-status's discipline: the
           branch_generations triggers are DEFERRED, so a gen read
           inside the tx that wrote sees the PRE value. branch/cp
           captures the branch's current generation AFTER prior
           writes commit — never inside a store tx holding pins.

           ==========================================================
           NOPALES SURFACE (server-only family, like the other
           ref-addressed branch prims)
           ==========================================================
             (branch/cp "name" [message])     -- capture on the CURRENT branch
             (branch/cp-list [owner/branch])  -- newest first, own or
                                               readable ref
             (branch/cp-diff "name")          -- per-pin classify between
                                               the captured gen and head;
                                               the branch_status decision
                                               table windowed, NOT a second
                                               classifier
             (branch/cp-restore "name" [names...]) -- restore the named
                                               pins (default: every pin
                                               whose head hash differs
                                               from its captured hash) to
                                               their captured bodies
             (branch/log [owner/branch] ["cp-name"]) -- the oplog: newest-
                                               first write timeline
                                               (name, kind, op, author,
                                               gen, changed_at) from the
                                               version trail + code_history;
                                               windowed by cp name, else
                                               the last 64 writes

           ==========================================================
           SEMANTICS
           ==========================================================
           RESTORE IS A WRITE, NOT A TIME MACHINE: restorations land
           as new writes at head gen (code_history before-images fire
           for free — nothing is silent; obituary rule), and the gen
           counter only moves forward.
           CODE ONLY: a checkpoint restores function/type/route pins.
           Data never rolls back (branches are CODE, not data —
           shared-data-spaces).
           NO BASE_GEN ENTANGLEMENT: a cp records gen, not a fork
           point. branch/cp-restore does not move base_gen (that is
           merge's move); a restored branch stays genuinely diverged
           rather than fake-synced.
           RECOVERABLE BY CONSTRUCTION: cp/restore of a pin another
           member changed after the cp is never the only copy — the
           clobbered body lives in code_history exactly like a merge
           clobber.

           ==========================================================
           NOT BUILT YET (named here, not in implements):
           migrations/0NN_branch_checkpoints.sql,
           image/lib/store_checkpoints.ml (rides store_history),
           agent/SSH surface unchanged — pure image prims.
           ==========================================================
      |))
     )
     (verify
      (build "dune build image")
      (test "image/test/checkpoints_test.ml — cp captures post-commit head gen; cp-list newest first; cp/restore fires one code_history row per changed pin; restore leaves base_gen untouched")
      (smoke "(branch/cp \"before\") after defines; more defines; (branch/cp-diff \"before\") lists the diverged worklist; (branch/cp-restore \"before\") restores and reports per name")
      (smoke "(branch/log) renders newest-first writes with author + gen, windowed by cp name"))
    )
  (subsection ast-nodes
   (doc "Design: addressable AST nodes as substrate for surgical edits and AI patches")
   (status planned)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-16 design pass (ralph loop 19): nodal addressing + three-way merge are now fixed; materialized nodes table deferred. Re-filed issue BORGE-1786925842. 2026-08-02: design-only proposal; function_defs remain whole-body JSONB.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         TODAY: two trees, two models
         - Branch tree: rows in branches, fork/merge,
           recursive CTE lookup, unit = whole function
         - AST tree: JSONB blob in function_defs.body,
           in-memory eval walk, no node ids, edit =
           replace whole body

         DESIGN (2026-08-16, ralph loop 19, BORGE-1786925842):
         Give AST nodes ADDRESSABILITY so tools can patch
         subexpressions and a genuine THREE-WAY MERGE can be
         computed, reusing the diff spine already shipped
         (branching.diff / lisp-interpreter.ast-diff). Kept
         cheap: identity is a PATH computed on the fly, NOT a
         materialized table. A materialized nodes table stays
         OPTIONAL and DEFERRED (see non-goals).

         ========= 1. NODE ADDRESSING (no table) =========
         A node inside a whole-expr body is addressed by a
         PATH from the root, in the same vocabulary the
         ast_diff walk already labels its children with:
           positional: if.test if.then if.else
                       lambda.params lambda.body
                       let.name let.val let.body
                       define.name define.val
                       seq.a seq.b quote.body
           list:       app.fn app.arg[i] prim.arg[i]
                       letrec.binding[NAME] lambda.param[i]
         Example path: lambda.body.app.arg[1].app.fn.var
         Identity = (row key: branch_id, name) + path.
         Pure helpers in lisp/lib make nodes addressable with
         NO schema change (function bodies stay whole-expr
         JSONB):
           select_path  : expr -> path -> expr option
           replace_path : path -> expr -> expr -> expr

         ========= 2. PATCH REPRESENTATION =========
         A two-way diff (base -> target) flattens to a set of
         atomic path-keyed edits, exactly what Ast_diff.delta
         already produces labelled by the child paths above:
           Replace(path, from, to)   node at path replaced
           Insert(path, idx, expr)   list-child insert
           Delete(path, idx)         list-child remove
         The Replaced <from=absent> sentinel in ast_diff
         already encodes Insert/Delete at list positions. A
         patch is therefore diff(base,target) PLUS the path
         vocabulary - no new algorithm, only the missing
         path->node selector from part 1.

         ========= 3. THREE-WAY MERGE (base, ours, theirs)
                  the algorithm tree-time (campaign 5) and
                  local-first sync (campaign 8) both reuse
            =========
           ours   = diff(base, ours)
           theirs = diff(base, theirs)
         Group edits by node path. Disjoint paths compose
         with no cross-order. A same path edited by BOTH:
           - identical target  -> keep once (both agreed)
           - different target  -> CONFLICT: report path + ours
             value + theirs value; v1 resolution = keep-ours
             + report (consistent with branching.merge's
             report-only-conflict philosophy); v2 may offer
             3-way resolution.
         Apply the merged edits to base -> merged body, then
         re-validate (ast_hash equality / eval smoke). A
         merge always has a base: the fork point, branches.
         last_synced, or a code_history before-image.

         ========= 4. MATERIALIZED NODES TABLE (optional, deferred)
            =========
         For SURGICAL in-place patching of one huge function
         (AI rewrites a subexpression without rewriting the
         whole JSONB), a materialized
           nodes(id, branch_id, kind, payload, parent_id,
                 position, ...)
         table pays off - derived/mirrored from the expr on
         write (like the tree_nodes mirrors), keyed by
         (branch_id, name, path). DEFERRED: the non-goal
         below (whole-function propose/edit ships first) says
         it is not needed yet, and it adds schema + mirror +
         writer complexity.

         ========= 5. LAYERING =========
         P1 (this substrate): pure path addressing +
             replace_path + merge3 in lisp/lib, testable with
             dune runtest lisp (no Postgres).
         P2: wire into Store.merge_branch - replace the naive
             full-copy with diff-based merge + a conflict
             report using merge3 (this is branching.merge's
             open TODO).
         P3 (deferred): materialized nodes table for surgical
             in-place patching.

         IMPLEMENTED so far: the diff spine this design
         consumes (branching.diff + lisp-interpreter.ast-diff)
         is shipped (module ast_diff, /diff page, (diff from
         to) prim). This subsection fixes the AST-node
         addressing + merge3 design those consumers build on;
         the P1 merge3 module and P2 merge wire-in are the
         remaining make work (see BORGE-1786925842).

         Non-goals for first product cut (unchanged):
         - Do not block function-editor or ai-authoring
           tools on this redesign
         - Whole-function propose/edit ships first
         - No alpha-equivalence / rename detection in the
           merge (inherits the diff spine non-goals)
    |))
   )
  )
 )


 (section branching-ast-ergonomics
  (doc "DECISION (roerick 2026-10-06): branching operations are AST-aware,
   never line-based. The unit of versioning is already the define (pin);
   the unit of DIFF and MERGE becomes the SUBTREE of the define's
   s-expression, compared in canonical pp_expr form (whitespace-
   normalized). Three-way rule per subtree position: one side changed ->
   take it; both changed identically -> keep; both changed differently ->
   conflict, reported at the SUBTREE PATH (e.g. workstreams/pulse-page :
   body : (list/map ...) — never a line hunk, never a whole-define
   clobber). Hash = the canonical-sexp hash (the pin-hygiene hash) — no
   text lines anywhere in the mechanism.")
   (status partial)
  (* agent note (|2026-10-07 ralph loop (branch ralph/spec-branching-rework): the 2026-10-06 draft restructured into book forms — the nested (section ast-diff / ast-merge / debts) drafts became subsections sexp-diff / ast-merge / branch-ops, each (status planned) with a details block preserving the draft text + a verify block (branch-status exemplar style). The OPS TABLE and GRANULARITY paragraphs moved from this section's details into branch-ops details; the debts list folded into branch-ops details too. roerick's DECISION doc above is VERBATIM, untouched. implements lines name only files that exist (the diff spine ast_diff.ml, the algebra branch_algebra.ml); unbuilt modules are named in details text instead. The section status 'draft' (not a valid borge status — lint flagged it) became 'planned', matching its subsections. 'branches are for CODE, not data' framing + shared-data-spaces untouched (T6, blocked on roerick).|) *)
   (* agent note (|2026-10-12 overnight reconcile (branching rework): statuses flipped against the built substrate. sexp-diff -> implemented (T1, commit e5d030a); ast-merge -> implemented (8922851 merge ralph/ast-merge-store, plus 6fe8dac list frame rule); branch-ops -> partial (status/pull/push + the checkpoint family built; tree face, delete/rename, branch/land still open). Section planned -> partial to match its subsections.|) *)
  (subsection sexp-diff
   (doc "Pure structural diff of two exprs into a FLAT path-keyed edit script; apply round-trips the script")
    (status implemented)
    (implements lisp/lib/ast_diff.ml lisp/lib/sexp_diff.ml lisp/lib/ast_path.ml lisp/test/sexp_diff_test.ml)
    (* agent note (|2026-10-12 BUILT (T1, commit e5d030a): lisp/lib/ast_path.ml, select_path/replace_path over the child-path vocabulary the walk already labels (if.test/then/else, lambda.params/body, letrec.binding[NAME], app.arg[i], ...); lisp/lib/sexp_diff.ml, the Ast_diff walk (same/modified/replaced + list-LCS) flattened into the flat path-keyed Replace/Insert/Delete edit script; apply replays it sequence-safely (a mid-sequence insert does NOT shift following siblings' paths). No second comparison algorithm, no schema change. Tests: lisp/test/sexp_diff_test.ml round-trip property apply(diff(a,b),a)=b over all 11 expr ctors + generated pairs against a naive reference. Build + tests green.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         sexp_diff.ml: pure structural diff on canonical sexps. Edit
         script at subtree granularity. Round-trip property: applying the
         script to the parent AST yields the child AST. Property-tested
         against a naive reference (the branch_algebra pattern).

         SHAPE (ast-nodes part 2): diff(a,b) flattens the Ast_diff walk
         (same/modified/replaced + list-LCS) into a FLAT path-keyed edit
         script — Replace(path, from, to) | Insert(path, idx, expr) |
         Delete(path, idx) — in the same child-path vocabulary the walk
         already labels (if.test/then/else, lambda.params/body, let.name/
         val/body, define.name/val, seq.a/b, quote.body, app.fn,
         app.arg[i], prim.arg[i], letrec.binding[NAME], lambda.param[i]).
         apply : edits -> expr -> expr replays the script onto the base
         expr; a mid-sequence insert must NOT shift following siblings'
         paths. The node selector it needs ships beside it in lisp/lib:
         ast_path.ml (select_path / replace_path) — no second comparison
         algorithm, no schema change.
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build")
    (test "lisp/test/sexp_diff_test.ml — round-trip property apply(diff(a,b), a) = b over all 11 expr ctors and generated expr pairs against a naive reference (branch_algebra test pattern)")
    (test "a mid-sequence insert does NOT shift following siblings' paths")
    (smoke "diff+apply reuse Ast_diff's walk decisions (same/modified/replaced + list-LCS) — no second comparison algorithm")))
  (subsection ast-merge
   (doc "Three-way merge at subtree granularity; conflicts are DATA in the result, never silently resolved")
    (status implemented)
    (implements lisp/lib/sexp_merge.ml lisp/lib/sexp_diff.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/session_control_prims.ml lisp/test/sexp_merge_test.ml)
    (* agent note (|2026-10-12 BUILT (T5): lisp/lib/sexp_merge.ml, pure three-way merge at subtree granularity: disjoint path edits compose, both-identical applies once, both-different = CONFLICT at the exact subtree path, conflicts are DATA in the result (merged body + conflict list), never silently resolved, never landed in the tree (the jj divergence named above stands). 6fe8dac added the list frame rule: a shared edit never leaves the live side's frame. Store.merge_branch (image/lib/store.ml) routes diverged defines through the merge3 hook at the eval boundary (8922851, merge ralph/ast-merge-store); image/lib/session_control_prims.ml owns the eval-boundary side and the conflict worklist surface. Tests: lisp/test/sexp_merge_test.ml green.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Three-way merge at subtree granularity: base = the fork-point
         body; per subtree, one-side-changed applies, both-identical
         applies, both-changed-differently -> conflict at the subtree
         path. Diverged defines are NEVER auto-merged (merge_applies
         obituary rule, extended from pins to subtrees). Conflicts land
         in the conflict worklist face with the conflicting subtree
         rendered.

         MECHANISM (ast-nodes part 3): ours = diff(base,ours),
         theirs = diff(base,theirs) via sexp_diff; group edits by path;
         disjoint paths compose with no cross-order; same path edited by
         both: identical target -> keep once, different target ->
         CONFLICT at the subtree path with ours+theirs values. Conflicts
         are DATA in the result (merged body + conflict list) — never
         silently resolved. Re-validate the merged body (canonical-sexp
         hash). A merge always has a base: the fork point (base_gen via
         the branch-status read path), or a code_history before-image.
           DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM JJ, NAMED 2026-10-12 so nobody
           "fixes" it later: jj stores conflicts INSIDE revisions — a
           tree floats with markers until you resolve it. A
           Pricklypear branch is live evaluated state: a stored
           conflicted body 500s every route it serves, in every
           session that touches it. So the conflict NEVER lands in
           the tree: merge returns data, resolution happens in the
           worklist, and the branch holds resolved bodies only.

    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build")
    (test "lisp/test/sexp_merge_test.ml — disjoint edits both present; both-identical applied once; both-different conflicts at the exact path; delete-vs-edit; nested lambda/letrec")
    (test "property: merge(base, ours=base, theirs) = theirs exactly, and symmetrically")
    (smoke "a conflict is reported with the exact subtree path + ours/theirs values, target unchanged")))
  (subsection branch-ops
   (doc "The ergonomics layer over branch_algebra.ml: status/pull/push/tree/delete/rename faces + prims, every surface rendered from the store")
    (status partial)
    (implements image/lib/branch_algebra.ml image/lib/branch_push.ml image/lib/session_control_prims.ml image/test/branch_push_test.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         OPS TABLE — the ergonomics layer over branch_algebra.ml. The
         algebra computes everything (classify:
         clean/ahead-new/ahead/behind/diverged/synced; chain; pin
         hygiene); nothing surfaces it:
           clone   -> fork (exists) + agents boot into <parent>/agent
                      (exists)
           status  -> NEW face+prim (branch/status ref): per-pin
                      classify + counts + the divergent worklist. Closes
                      the /branches stale-snapshot bug: rendered from the
                      store, not the session snapshot (2026-10-05/06
                      incidents).
           pull    -> NEW (branch/pull ref): every BEHIND pin adopts the
                      parent's version (bulk unoverride) + report;
                      diverged pins untouched (the obituary rule — no
                      silent reversals).
           push    -> NEW (branch/push ref [names]): agent-promote to
                      the PARENT branch — the promote_to_personal
                      generalization, gated by the parent's
                      allow_agent_promote. Dependency closure runs FIRST:
                      the holes check on the target — a promoted pin
                      referencing a name unbound on the target is refused
                      or auto-included (2026-10-05 incident:
                      pulse-row-from promoted without
                      workstreams/stale-node? -> system/main 500).
           tree    -> NEW face: the forest — parents, children,
                      per-edge (ahead/behind/diverged) counts. One page.
           delete / rename -> NEW prims + UI (a 0-own-code branch could
                      not be removed; renames were impossible).
           land     -> NEW prim (branch/land pkg name...): apply vault
                      bodies to live main — the REPL form of pp-sync
                      land, admin-gated + audited. SCOPE AMENDMENT
                      (2026-10-07, found by roerick: a body fix required
                      a bash incantation on the build box — the
                      vault-authority model was scoped but its surface
                      was CLI-only; an op the authority model requires
                      must be a repl op).

         GRANULARITY: the pin stays the unit of versioning; the AST
         makes the DIFF subtree-granular inside a pin and makes
         RENAME/MOVE detectable (hash-equal body under a new name =
         rename, not delete+add). pull_set is exactly the BEHIND pins
         (per the classify decision table) that branch/pull would
         adopt-parent; diverged/ahead/synced/clean NEVER in the pull
         set.

         DEBTS folded into this work: the /branches page renders
         session-snapshot counters (stale) — must read the store; the
         promote body-corruption incident (2026-10-05: promoted copies
         500'd where the in-session originals rendered — root-cause the
         promote's store write); the /branches display shows two rows
         for one name (branch-code row vs garden row) — needs an
         explicit label.
    |))
   )
      (* agent note (|2026-10-12 branch/push BUILT (the push row above): target = <ref-owner>/personal — the promote_to_personal target generalized over any ref the caller controls; the branch-GRAPH parent of a workshop is system/main itself, so branch/push refuses system/* and promote-to-main stays the admin-gated path. Callers: the ref's owner human (own garden, no gate — the branch-faces decision flow in auth.borg), the parent-linked agent user (allow_agent_promote gate, same as promote), or admin. Closure FIRST via Call_graph.push_closure — the holes App-Var analytic over the source chain's effective defs: kernel prims + special forms + target-visible names are bound; an unbound ref auto-includes from the source's OWN rows or the push REFUSES WHOLE (complete hole report; never a partial landing — the 2026-10-05 pulse-row-from class); inherited-only names refuse fork-first (promote_functions' own-row lookup would silently skip); default set = depth-0 own code; writes via promote_functions (atomic, routes travel, package stamp preserved). Lands: Store.list_function_names_meta_at, Call_graph.push_closure, Branch_push.push, session_control_prims "branch/push", effect.ml write_db floor. Tests: image/test/branch_push_test.ml.|) *)
      (* agent note (|2026-10-12 reconcile of the OPS TABLE: status (branch/status, a2d7a44) and pull (branch/pull, unbind-path bulk adopt, a2d7a44) BUILT; push BUILT (note below); the checkpoint family (branch/cp, branch/cp-list, branch/cp-diff, branch/cp-restore + the branch/log face, migration 040) BUILT (see branching.checkpoints). Still OPEN, why this subsection is (status partial): the tree face (one-page forest with per-edge ahead/behind/diverged counts), delete/rename prims + UI, and the branch/land prim (vault bodies onto live main, the REPL form of pp-sync land).|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image/test/branch_algebra_test.exe && ./_build/default/image/test/branch_algebra_test.exe")
    (test "image/test/branch_algebra_test.ml — rename detection: hash-equal body under a new name = RENAMED(from,to); same-name-different-body vs different-name-same-body discriminate delete+add from rename")
    (test "image/test/branch_algebra_test.ml — pull_set = exactly the BEHIND pins of classify on a randomized forest; diverged/ahead/synced/clean never in the pull set (naive reference)")
      (smoke "(branch/status ref) renders per-pin classify + counts + the divergent worklist from Store.branch_status — the ONE classifier, read from the store, never the session snapshot")
      (smoke "(branch/pull ref) adopts the parent's version on every BEHIND pin via the unbind path (never a body copy — pin hygiene); diverged pins untouched; returns the pulled + skipped-diverged lists")
      (test "image/test/branch_push_test.ml — push_closure: target-visible/kernel/special-form refs are free; an unbound ref auto-includes from the source (discovery order, mutual recursion safe) or holes its carrier; holes refuse the push all-or-nothing with a complete report; an explicit dep lands in pushed, never auto")
      (test "image/test/branch_push_test.ml — store push: a pierced set (entry + broken->absent) refuses WHOLE and the target stays untouched; a clean push lands entry + auto deps verbatim; a name inherited by both sides stays a chain view on the target, never an own row; an inherited-only name refuses fork-first; the default set is depth-0 own code")
      (smoke "(branch/push \"gregor/agent\" entry) by gregor (or the parent-linked agent when allow_agent_promote) lands entry + auto-included callees on gregor/personal via promote_functions (routes travel); a push with a dep unbound everywhere is refused with the exact hole list — no partial landing"))))
   (subsection op-transplant
    (doc "Op-level transplant: pick named writes out of a branch's gen stream and land them on another branch as new writes — cherry-pick over the log")
    (status planned)
    (* agent note (|2026-10-16 spec'd in plan phase (roerick + AL VC-reinvention conversation). The dig showed capture is DONE (tree_versions append-only + code_history before-images + branch/log + the checkpoint family, 2026-10-12) and merge/promote/push are whole-branch or dep-closure granularity. The missing primitive is the OP-LEVEL CURSOR: branch/log is a fold, not a worklist you can act on across branches. This subsection is that cursor. Sibling: file-bridge (files as projections). ast-nodes P3 (subtree pick) stays deferred.|) *)
    (details
     (doc (|
            ==========================================================
            WHY
            ==========================================================
            An agent hot-defines six fns on admin/personal from a
            phone; the operator wants three of them on system/main
            tonight. Today's surfaces: promote (whole set), branch/push
            (dep closure to the owner's personal), merge (whole
            branch), cp-restore (SAME branch only). Nothing moves
            NAMED OPS across branches. cp-diff already computes the
            worklist shape (per-pin classify between a captured gen
            and head); this subsection makes the worklist ACTIONABLE
            cross-branch.

            ==========================================================
            NOPALES SURFACE (server-only, ref-addressed,
            anonymous-denied — the checkpoint family's rules)
            ==========================================================
              (branch/pick target-ref name...)          -- source =
                 CURRENT branch, bodies at source head
              (branch/pick target-ref name... 'at "cp") -- bodies as
                 captured at the source checkpoint
              (branch/pick target-ref name... 'gen N)   -- bodies as
                 of gen N (the code_history before-image walk)
            Result is DATA: landed / skipped(name reason) / clobbers —
            never a silent partial.

            ==========================================================
            SEMANTICS
            ==========================================================
            RESTORE-IS-A-WRITE (checkpoints discipline, unchanged):
            picked bodies land through the tree write helpers at the
            TARGET head gen — version trail + code_history
            before-images fire for free, gen only moves forward,
            target base_gen untouched. The pick is itself a log entry
            on the target (op = pick, source ref recorded — the origin
            column below), so a bad pick is undone by picking the
            before-image back; no special revert path.

            CLOBBER GUARD: a target own pin whose hash differs from
            the picked body is DIVERGED. Divergence is never silently
            overwritten (branching.merge philosophy): by default the
            name lands in skipped with reason diverged-target + both
            hashes; 'force lands it (before-image in code_history, and
            the log entry naming the source satisfies the obituary
            rule).

            CLOSURE FIRST (branch/push discipline; the 2026-10-05
            pulse-row-from class): Call_graph.push_closure runs over
            the picked set against the target chain; an unbound ref
            auto-includes from the source's OWN rows or refuses the
            pick WHOLE with the complete hole report. Never a partial
            landing.

            AUTH (branch/push discipline): the target ref's owner
            human, the parent-linked agent under allow_agent_promote,
            or admin. Targets under system/* are ADMIN-ONLY
            (promote-to-main stays the gated main path). The source
            ref must be caller-readable (branch/status's rule).

            SOURCE RESOLUTION for 'at/'gen: the name's own pin at that
            gen (tree_versions window), else the code_history
            before-image walk, else ABSENT — ABSENT is a value, not an
            error: a pick of a name absent at the cp is an unbind
            request and REFUSES by default (unbind is a separate
            explicit op).

            LOG ORIGIN (one additive migration serves this subsection
            and file-bridge): the version trail gains an origin column
            (default 'eval'; 'pick', 'file', 'restore' as those paths
            land) + a detail text (source ref / file name). branch/log
            renders it.

            ==========================================================
            NON-GOALS
            ==========================================================
            - Range/revset algebra (cp window + explicit names only)
            - Subtree-level pick (ast-nodes P3, deferred)
            - Datum rows (branches are CODE, not data)
            - Rename following (name = identity; ast-merge's
              hash-equal rename detection is a later reuse)
|))
    )
    (verify
     (build "dune build image")
     (test "image/test/op_transplant_test.ml — pick lands bodies as new writes at target head gen; one code_history before-image per clobbered target pin; target base_gen untouched; the pick itself appears in branch/log with the source ref recorded")
     (test "image/test/op_transplant_test.ml — diverged target pin: default skips with diverged-target + both hashes; 'force lands and reports; absent-at-cp refuses (unbind stays explicit)")
     (test "image/test/op_transplant_test.ml — closure: a picked body referencing a name unbound on the target chain refuses WHOLE with the complete hole report; auto-include lands entry + dep verbatim")
     (smoke "agent defines a b c on personal; (branch/cp \"one\"); defines d e; admin (branch/pick \"system/main\" \"a\" \"b\" 'at \"one\") lands exactly a b; (branch/log \"system/main\") shows the two picks with the source ref recorded")))
   (subsection file-bridge
    (doc "Files as PROJECTIONS of the def store: a local file is a manifest of defs + raw spans; editing it and eval'ing in-image are the same op stream")
    (status planned)
    (* agent note (|2026-10-16 spec'd in plan phase (roerick + AL VC-reinvention conversation). THE INVENTION in that conversation: everything else sketched already exists (log/checkpoints/merge3/pin-hygiene/op-transplant). A file stops being a mirror with sync semantics and becomes a VIEW of the branch: parse -> def-level ops through the same write helpers eval uses; materialize -> regenerate with raw spans preserved. The pp-local lens is the transport (server stays write authority — the local-first framing is unchanged). VAULT DEMOTION below is a DECISION CANDIDATE flagged for roerick: vault-authority (2026-10-02) is locked and this subsection amends it.|) *)
    (details
     (doc (|
            ==========================================================
            WHY
            ==========================================================
            branching.vault-authority made libs/*.pp a generated
            MIRROR and killed hand-editing as a deploy path — correct,
            but it left local development with no first-class story:
            eval-over-HTTP per form, or edit a file that is dead text
            to the habitat. The bridge closes that: the file is a
            PROJECTION of the branch, so a local edit and an in-image
            define are the same event type — a def-level op through
            the tree write helpers. One op stream, two doors.

            ==========================================================
            PROJECTION FORMAT (one file per package, the libs/*.pp
            shape)
            ==========================================================
            Header manifest, a leading comment block the parser owns:
              ;;; branch: admin/dev   base-gen: 412   projection: v1
            Then per def, in manifest order:
            - the canonical body (printed pp_expr form)
            - zero or more RAW SPANS attached to that def's slot:
              comments, reader conditionals, non-def top-level forms.
              Raw spans are OWNED BY THE FILE: the image never
              generates them, the projection never drops them.

            ROUND-TRIP PROPERTIES (property-tested):
              parse(materialize(branch)) = branch def set, spans empty
              materialize(parse(file))   = the file, spans byte-equal

            ==========================================================
            MECHANISM
            ==========================================================
            FILE-TO-IMAGE (ingest): parse locally; diff the def set
            against the manifest's recorded base (per-name hash
            equality, ABSENT as its own value — the branch/status
            decision discipline); emit define / redefine / unbind ops.
            ONE server call lands the batch (candidate prim name
            branch/ingest; the make phase fixes the name): it
            validates base-gen freshness, runs the clobber guard and
            closure (op-transplant's rules, same code), then writes
            through the tree write helpers — version trail +
            code_history fire, log entries record origin=file + the
            file name. Head moved past base-gen: the conflict set is
            the names whose head hash differs from BOTH the file body
            and the base body — reported + skipped, the rest land;
            'force lands all with before-images.

            IMAGE-TO-FILE (materialize): regenerate canonical bodies;
            re-attach raw spans by slot identity (name); spans of
            since-unbound defs stay as tombstone comments until an
            explicit 'prune-spans.

            TRANSPORT: the pp-local lens (borge/local-first.borg).
            The server stays index + write authority exactly as in
            data sync; ingest is the one eval call carrying the op
            batch, never a per-define HTTP loop.

            ==========================================================
            VAULT DEMOTION (DECISION CANDIDATE — needs roerick; amends
            the locked branching.vault-authority 2026-10-02)
            ==========================================================
            When P2 lands: libs/*.pp becomes EXPORT-ONLY (pp-sync
            snapshot unchanged); pp-sync land retires into the
            branch/land REPL op (the branch-ops scope amendment); the
            additive boot seed shrinks to cold-start-of-empty-habitat.
            UNTIL RATIFIED: the bridge targets personal/dev branches
            only and the vault keeps its current semantics — the two
            never write the same branch in one workflow.

            ==========================================================
            TRADEOFFS NAMED (so nobody fixes them later)
            ==========================================================
            - Bodies print CANONICALLY. Author formatting is not
              preserved; comments and reader gunk survive only as raw
              spans. The pin-hygiene hash domain is canonical-sexp —
              the file cannot carry a second formatting domain without
              breaking body-equality reasoning everywhere.
            - Name = identity. Renames are delete+add in the op stream
              (ast-merge's hash-equal rename detection is a later
              reuse).

            ==========================================================
            NON-GOALS
            ==========================================================
            - Non-Lisp languages (blob-tier; the OCaml kernel stays
              git — two node tiers, one store is a later STORAGE
              story, not this subsection)
            - A bidirectional watcher daemon (v1 is explicit
              ingest/materialize; a watcher is glochid-side
              convenience later)
            - Subtree-granular file diffs (the pin stays the unit;
              ast-nodes P3 deferred)

            ==========================================================
            PHASES
            ==========================================================
              P1: projection format + materialize + round-trip
                  properties — pure, lisp/lib, dune runtest lisp, no
                  Postgres.
              P2: ingest server call + conflict report + origin=file
                  log entries (image/lib, rides store_history; shares
                  the origin-column migration with op-transplant).
              P3: vault demotion (blocked on the roerick decision).
|))
    )
    (verify
     (build "dune build")
     (test "lisp/test/file_projection_test.ml — round-trip: materialize(parse(file)) preserves every raw span byte-identically and the def set in manifest order; parse(materialize(branch)) yields exactly the branch def set")
     (test "image/test/file_bridge_test.ml — ingest lands one op per changed def through the tree write helpers (version trail + code_history rows exist, origin=file); unchanged file against an unmoved base = zero ops")
     (test "image/test/file_bridge_test.ml — head moved past base-gen: diverged names report with hashes and skip; the rest land; 'force lands all with before-images")
     (smoke "materialize admin/dev to a file; edit two bodies + add a comment locally; ingest: (branch/log \"admin/dev\") shows exactly two origin=file ops; re-materialize and the comment survives")))
)
